//! An embedded key-value store that maps bytes to bytes, stored in files in a directory under an
//! `fs` root.
//!
//! A store is a log. A commit appends one frame containing every key it puts or deletes, so a
//! batch is either complete or absent. Each batch takes the next sequence number, which becomes
//! the version of every key it wrote. A program's handle on an open store is a `Store` value: the
//! index, a `Map` from each key to where its value is, and where the log ends. A commit returns
//! the next `Store` value, a `Snapshot` is the index at one point in time, and a read is one
//! `fs.read_at` checked against the checksum in the index. So reads through an old snapshot see
//! no later write. Use this when one program keeps keyed bytes next to itself and needs them back
//! after a crash; use `std.sqlite` when the data needs more than one kind of query.
//!
//! `open` takes the directory's lock and returns the store, and `close` releases the lock: they
//! are the pair to pass to `with_scope`. The lock is `fs.lock`'s file, which a later locker removes
//! once it is thirty seconds old. So the lock file stores its holder's token and the position
//! where its log ends, and every commit reads it back and writes it again, as a compaction does
//! between its records. A store that is in use is therefore never mistaken for a dead one. A store
//! that sat idle past that age and had its lock taken finds another token and fails with
//! `LockLost`. A `Store` value that is not the latest one a commit returned finds a different log
//! position and fails with `Stale`. Both checks happen before anything is written. So a store has
//! one writer, which uses its `Store` values in the order its commits returned them, and tasks
//! that share a store commit one at a time (`std.task`'s `with_permit`). This cannot detect a
//! locker that removes the lock in the instant between its holder's read and write. But a frame
//! written anywhere other than where its writer expected the log to end does not verify, so what
//! such a pair appends is cut off by the next `open` and never read as a batch.
//!
//! Each commit chooses its durability. `Durable` syncs the log before it returns, so a power loss
//! afterwards keeps the commit. `Fast` returns once the frame is appended: every reader sees it,
//! but a power loss may lose it along with every commit since the last durable one. A power loss
//! never loses a commit without also losing every commit after it.
//!
//! The file formats follow. Every number is little-endian, and sizes are in bytes.
//!
//! A segment, `NNNNNNNN.log`, numbered from 1, is a 64-byte header followed by frames. The header
//! is `PLYKVLOG`; the format, 1, in four bytes; four bytes of flags; the segment's number in
//! eight; the sequence number of the last batch committed before its first frame, in eight; and
//! the 32 bytes of the store's shape, all zeros for a store of raw bytes. Flag bit 0 marks the
//! segment as a base: a segment a compaction wrote in full, which replaces every segment numbered
//! below it.
//!
//! A frame is a 24-byte head and a body. The head is `F1 6B 76 0A`; the body's length in four; an
//! eight-byte checksum; and the mark in eight. The checksum is the first eight bytes of the BLAKE3
//! of the frame's offset in its segment (in eight), the length, the mark and the body. The mark is
//! the offset up to which the segment was known to be synced when the frame was written. The body
//! is the batch's sequence number in eight, or zero in a base; the number of records in four; and
//! the records. A record is its kind in one byte (1 a put, 2 a delete, 3 a value a compaction
//! kept); the key's length in four and the value's in four; for kind 3, the value's version in
//! eight; the key; and the value. A base contains only frames of kept values, ends with a frame of
//! no records, and is never appended to.
//!
//! A hint, `NNNNNNNN.hint`, summarizes a full segment's frames, so opening reads the hint instead
//! of the segment. It is `PLYKVHNT`; the format in four; four zero bytes; the segment's number in
//! eight; the segment's length in eight; the sequence number after its last batch in eight; the
//! number of entries in eight; the size of the segment's records in eight, each counted as 17 plus
//! its key and value; the entries; and an eight-byte checksum over everything before. An entry is
//! 1 for a value or 2 for a deleted key; the version in eight; the value's offset in its segment in
//! eight; its length in four; the first eight bytes of its BLAKE3; the key's length in four; and
//! the key.
//!
//! `lock` contains the 32 characters of its holder's token. Once the store is open, it also
//! contains the number of the last segment and that segment's length as the last commit expected
//! to leave them, eight bytes each.
//!
//! Opening finds the newest base and removes what it replaced. It reads each full segment through
//! its hint, or frame by frame if the hint is missing or belongs to a different segment, and reads
//! the last segment frame by frame. In the last segment, a frame that does not verify, with no
//! later frame whose mark is past it, is a write that never finished, and the log is cut there.
//! Anything else is an error that names the file and the offset, and nothing is skipped: a frame
//! that does not verify elsewhere, a frame out of sequence, a base that does not end properly, or
//! a header that does not belong to this store. Damage to the frames at the end of the log as of
//! its last sync, with nothing written after, is the one loss that looks like an unfinished write.
//! A segment that reaches `Limits.file` is synced, given its hint, and followed by the next one.
//! `compact` writes every live value into one new base next to the log and renames it into place
//! as `fs::write_atomic` does. So a crash leaves either the old segments or the new base, never
//! neither. The replaced segments stay on disk for the snapshots that read them, until `discard`
//! or the next `open` removes them.

import std.bin
import std.bin (BinCodec)
import std.bytes (u32_le, u64_le)
import std.fs
import std.hash (checksum)
import std.fs (fs, files, Durability, Durable, Fast, MemFs, MemResult, mem_crashed, mem_empty,
               mem_exists, mem_list, mem_read, mem_remove, mem_size, mem_write, on_mem,
               on_mem_failing)
import std.io
import std.io (Sink)
import std.laws (refines)
import std.list
import std.option
import std.path (join)
import std.random (entropy, rand, token_hex, with_rand)
import std.seq
import std.seq (Seq, seq)
import std.string
import std.gen

// --- Limits, errors and values ----------------------------------------------------

/// A store's limits: the most bytes in a key, in a value, and in all the keys and values of one
/// batch, and how many bytes a segment may reach before the next one is started. A key is at
/// least one byte. The batch limit is at least the largest key and value together, and at most
/// what one read returns (64 MiB), since a frame is written and read whole.
pub type Limits = new { key: Int, value: Int, batch: Int, file: Int }

/// When a store compacts.
pub type Compaction =
  /// When `compact` is called, and never otherwise.
  | Manual
  /// During the commit that leaves this percentage of the log dead, once the dead part is at least
  /// this many bytes. The commit returns after the compaction.
  | WhenDead(Int, Int)

/// Which part of a commit exceeded its store's limits.
pub type Part = | Key | Value | Batch

/// Why a store operation failed.
pub type Error =
  /// Another store has the directory's lock.
  | Locked
  /// The lock no longer belongs to this store: it sat past the age at which a lock is considered
  /// dead and another store took it, or its own scope ended. Nothing was written.
  | LockLost
  /// The `Store` value is behind its log. Either another value of the same store committed or
  /// compacted after it, and that newer value can still commit, or an operation through it raised
  /// `Failed` after it began to write, and the store must be opened again. Nothing was committed.
  | Stale
  /// A file does not contain what this module wrote: the file, and the offset where reading
  /// stopped.
  | Corrupt(String, Int)
  /// A file the store refers to does not exist: a segment between two others, or one a snapshot
  /// reads that `discard` or a later `open` removed.
  | Missing(String)
  /// The store was written in another format.
  | OtherFormat(Int)
  /// The store was written under another shape: with different codecs from the ones it is opened
  /// with.
  | OtherShape
  /// A key that is empty or longer than the limit, a value longer than the limit, or a batch
  /// whose keys and values together exceed the limit.
  | Unfit(Part)
  /// A value's bytes are not something the codec it is read with could have written.
  | Undecodable(String)
  /// The file system failed: the path, and why.
  | Failed(String, fs::FileError)

/// What a store raises.
pub effect key_value {
  /// The store could not do what was requested.
  raise fail(error_of: Error)
}

fn fail<a>(error_of: Error) -> a / {key_value.fail} = key_value.fail(error_of)

/// One step of a batch: a value put under a key, or a key deleted.
pub type Op = | Put(Bytes, Bytes) | Delete(Bytes)

/// Where a value is stored: the batch that wrote it, its segment, its offset and length there,
/// and the first eight bytes of its BLAKE3.
pub type Slot = { version: Int, segment: Int, at: Int, len: Int, checksum: Bytes }

/// An open store, as one commit left it. `sequence` is the number of the last batch, `base` and
/// `active` are its first and last segments, `end` is the active segment's length, and `synced` is
/// how much of that is known to be synced. `total` is the size of the records in its segments,
/// `live` the size of those the index still refers to, and `weight` the size of those in the
/// active segment. `touched` records each key the active segment wrote, and whether it was put.
/// `replaced` lists the segments a compaction left for the snapshots that read them.
pub opaque type Store = new {
  dir: String,
  token: String,
  limits: Limits,
  policy: Compaction,
  shape: Bytes,
  index: Map<Bytes, Slot>,
  sequence: Int,
  base: Int,
  active: Int,
  end: Int,
  synced_bodies: Int,
  total: Int,
  live: Int,
  weight: Int,
  touched: Map<Bytes, Bool>,
  replaced: List<Int>,
}

/// A store's contents at one point in time, as a value that no later commit changes.
pub opaque type Snapshot = new { dir: String, index: Map<Bytes, Slot> }

/// One entry of a scan.
pub type Pair = { key: Bytes, value: Bytes, version: Int }

/// A compare-and-set's two results: the store afterwards, and whether the key had the expected
/// version.
pub type CompareAndSetResult = { store: Store, done: Bool }

/// A summary of a store: its number of keys; the size of the records in its log, each counted as
/// 17 plus its key and value; the size of those still in use, so the difference is what a
/// compaction drops; its number of segments; and the sequence number of its last batch.
pub type Stats = { entries: Int, total: Int, live: Int, segments: Int, sequence: Int }

// --- Names and bytes --------------------------------------------------------------

/// The format version this module writes, which every file's header contains.
pub fn format_version() -> Int = 1

/// The shape of a store of raw bytes, read with no codecs: 32 zero bytes.
pub fn raw() -> Bytes = b"\x00".repeat(32)

fn header_size() -> Int = 64
fn head_size() -> Int = 24
fn body_head_size() -> Int = 12
fn hint_header_size() -> Int = 56

/// How much a record counts toward a store's `total` and `live`: its size as a compaction writes
/// it.
fn record_size(key: Bytes, value: Int) -> Int = 17 + key.len() + value
fn frame_magic() -> Bytes = b"\xf1kv\n"
fn log_magic() -> Bytes = b"PLYKVLOG"
fn hint_magic() -> Bytes = b"PLYKVHNT"

/// The most bytes read from a file at once.
fn read_chunk_size() -> Int = 0x10_0000

/// The longest body a frame of a store with these limits has: a batch of one-byte keys.
fn longest_body(limits: Limits) -> Int = body_head_size() + 18 * limits.batch

fn digits(n: Int) -> String = n.to_string().pad_left(8, "0")
fn log_name(n: Int) -> String = digits(n) ++ ".log"
fn hint_name(n: Int) -> String = digits(n) ++ ".hint"
fn lock_path(dir: String) -> String = join(dir, "lock")

/// The number in a file's name before `suffix`, if the name is one this module would write.
fn file_number(name: String, suffix: String) -> Option<Int> / {abort.raise} =
  if !name.ends_with(suffix) { None } else {
    let stem = name.slice(0, name.len() - suffix.len());
    match Bytes::of_string(stem).int_of_ascii() {
      Some(n) -> if n > 0 && digits(n) == stem { Some(n) } else { None },
      None -> None,
    }
  }

fn read_u32(b: Bytes, at: Int) -> Int / {abort.raise} = b.u32_at(at).expect("four bytes")
fn read_u64(b: Bytes, at: Int) -> Int / {abort.raise} = b.u64_at(at).expect("eight bytes")

fn header_bytes(number: Int, base: Bool, before: Int, shape: Bytes) -> Bytes =
  Bytes::concat_all([
    log_magic(),
    u32_le(format_version()),
    u32_le(if base { 1 } else { 0 }),
    u64_le(number),
    u64_le(before),
    shape,
  ])

/// The frame of `body` at offset `at` in its segment. The checksum covers the offset, so the same
/// bytes at any other offset are not a valid frame.
fn frame(at: Int, synced: Int, body: Bytes) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} = {
  let length = u32_le(body.len());
  let mark = u64_le(synced);
  Bytes::concat_all([
    frame_magic(),
    length,
    checksum(Bytes::concat_all([u64_le(at), length, mark, body])),
    mark,
    body,
  ])
}

fn record(kind: Int, key: Bytes, version: Bytes, value: Bytes) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  Bytes::concat_all([
    byte_of_int(kind),
    u32_le(key.len()),
    u32_le(value.len()),
    version,
    key,
    value,
  ])

fn op_bytes(op: Op) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  match op {
    Put(key, value) -> record(1, key, b"", value),
    Delete(key) -> record(2, key, b"", b""),
  }

fn body_of(sequence: Int, records: List<Bytes>) -> Bytes =
  Bytes::concat_all([u64_le(sequence), u32_le(records.len()), Bytes::concat_all(records)])

// --- Reading a log ----------------------------------------------------------------

/// What reading frames builds: the index, the sequence number of the last batch read, the keys
/// the frames wrote, with whether each was put, and the size of their records.
type ReadState = { index: Map<Bytes, Slot>, sequence: Int, touched: Map<Bytes, Bool>, weight: Int }

/// `made` updated with the frame whose body is at `at` in segment `number`, in the file `name`.
/// The frame is the batch after the last one, or, in a base (when `kept` is true), a frame of kept
/// values.
fn apply_frame(
  state: ReadState,
  name: String,
  number: Int,
  at: Int,
  body: Bytes,
  is_base: Bool,
) -> ReadState / {key_value.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let size = body.len();
  if size < body_head_size() { fail(Corrupt(name, at)) } else {
    let sequence = read_u64(body, 0);
    let count = read_u32(body, 8);
    if sequence != (if is_base { 0 } else { state.sequence + 1 }) { fail(Corrupt(name, at)) } else {
      let read = iterate(
        { made: state, left: count, p: body_head_size() },
        count + 1,
        |r: { made: ReadState, left: Int, p: Int }|
          if r.left == 0 { Stop(r) }
          else if r.p + 9 > size { fail(Corrupt(name, at + r.p)) } else {
            let kind = body.at(r.p);
            let keyed = r.p + 9 + (if kind == 3 { 8 } else { 0 });
            let valued = keyed + read_u32(body, r.p + 1);
            let after = valued + read_u32(body, r.p + 5);
            let fits = if is_base { kind == 3 }
            else { kind == 1 || (kind == 2 && after == valued) };
            if !fits || valued == keyed || after > size { fail(Corrupt(name, at + r.p)) } else {
              let key = body.slice(keyed, valued);
              let slot = {
                version: if kind == 3 { read_u64(body, r.p + 9) } else { sequence },
                segment: number,
                at: at + valued,
                len: after - valued,
                checksum: checksum(body.slice(valued, after)),
              };
              Continue({
                made: {
                  index: if kind == 2 { r.made.index.remove(key) }
                  else { r.made.index.insert(key, slot) },
                  sequence: r.made.sequence,
                  touched: r.made.touched.insert(key, kind != 2),
                  weight: r.made.weight + record_size(key, after - valued),
                },
                left: r.left - 1,
                p: after,
              })
            }
          },
      );
      if read.p != size { fail(Corrupt(name, at + read.p)) }
      else { { ..read.made, sequence: if is_base { state.sequence } else { sequence } } }
    }
  }
}

/// Whether the bytes at `p` in `held`, which are at `at` in their segment, are one complete frame
/// of a store with these limits.
fn is_whole_frame(held: Bytes, p: Int, at: Int, limits: Limits) -> Bool / {abort.raise} = {
  let size = held.len();
  if p + head_size() > size || held.slice(p, p + 4) != frame_magic() { false } else {
    let length = read_u32(held, p + 4);
    let ends = p + head_size() + length;
    length <= longest_body(limits) && ends <= size
      && checksum(
        Bytes::concat_all([
          u64_le(at),
          held.slice(p + 4, p + 8),
          held.slice(p + 16, p + 24),
          held.slice(p + 24, ends),
        ]),
      )
      == held.slice(p + 8, p + 16)
  }
}

/// The state of a pass through a segment's frames: what they build, the offset of the next frame,
/// the bytes buffered from there, the file's length, whether the bytes at that offset are not a
/// frame, and whether the last frame read has no records.
type SegmentScan = { made: ReadState, at: Int, buffered: Bytes, size: Int, bad: Bool, closed: Bool }

/// Reads the frames of the segment at `path` from its header on, a piece of the file at a time,
/// and stops at its end or at the first bytes that are not a frame. `kept` means the segment is a
/// base.
fn scan_segment<[l]>(
  path: String,
  name: String,
  number: Int,
  size: Int,
  limits: Limits,
  start: ReadState,
  is_base: Bool,
) -> SegmentScan / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  iterate(
    { made: start, at: header_size(), buffered: b"", size: size, bad: false, closed: false },
    size *| 2 +| 16,
    |w: SegmentScan| {
      let have = w.buffered.len();
      let headed = have >= head_size();
      let wanted = if !headed { head_size() } else { head_size() + read_u32(w.buffered, 4) };
      let ended = w.at + have >= w.size;
      if ended && have == 0 { Stop(w) }
      else if headed
          && (!w.buffered.starts_with(frame_magic())
          || read_u32(w.buffered, 4) > longest_body(limits)) { Stop({ ..w, bad: true }) }
      else if have >= wanted {
        if !is_whole_frame(w.buffered, 0, w.at, limits) { Stop({ ..w, bad: true }) } else {
          Continue({
            ..w,
            made: apply_frame(
              w.made,
              name,
              number,
              w.at + head_size(),
              w.buffered.slice(head_size(), wanted),
              is_base,
            ),
            at: w.at + wanted,
            buffered: w.buffered.slice(wanted, have),
            closed: wanted == head_size() + body_head_size(),
          })
        }
      } else if ended { Stop({ ..w, bad: true }) } else {
        let from = w.at + have;
        let most = min(min(max(wanted - have, read_chunk_size()), w.size - from), 0x200_0000);
        match fs.read_at[l](path, from, most) {
          None -> fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("read_at"))),
          Some(more) -> if more.len() == 0 { Continue({ ..w, size: from }) }
          else { Continue({ ..w, buffered: w.buffered.concat(more) }) },
        }
      }
    },
  )

/// The bytes of the file at `path` from `from` to `to`, read a piece at a time.
fn read_range<[l]>(
  path: String,
  from: Int,
  to: Int,
) -> Bytes / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  Bytes::concat_all(
    range(0, (to - from + read_chunk_size() - 1) / read_chunk_size()).map(|i: Int|
      match fs.read_at[l](
          path,
          from + i * read_chunk_size(),
          min(read_chunk_size(), to - from - i * read_chunk_size()),
        ) {
        Some(bytes) -> bytes,
        None -> fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("read_at"))),
      }),
  )

/// Whether `tail`, the bytes after a frame at `bad` that did not verify, contains a complete frame
/// whose mark is past `bad`. Such a frame was written after a sync covered the bytes at `bad`, so
/// those bytes were then complete on disk, and their failure is damage, not an unfinished write.
fn has_synced_frame_after(tail: Bytes, bad: Int, limits: Limits) -> Bool / {abort.raise} =
  iterate(1, tail.len() + 2, |from: Int|
    match tail.index_of_from(frame_magic(), min(from, tail.len())) {
      None -> Stop(false),
      Some(p) -> if !is_whole_frame(tail, p, bad + p, limits) { Continue(p + 1) }
      else if read_u64(tail, p + 16) > bad { Stop(true) }
      else { Continue(p + head_size() + read_u32(tail, p + 4)) },
    })

/// A segment's header, checked to belong to this store: its number, its shape and this format.
type Header = { is_base: Bool, before: Int }

fn header_of<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  number: Int,
  shape: Bytes,
) -> Header / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let name = log_name(number);
  match fs.read_at[l](join(dir, name), 0, header_size()) {
    None -> fail(Missing(name)),
    Some(head) -> if head.len() < header_size() || !head.starts_with(log_magic()) {
      fail(Corrupt(name, 0))
    } else if read_u32(head, 8) != format_version() { fail(OtherFormat(read_u32(head, 8))) }
    else if read_u64(head, 16) != number { fail(Corrupt(name, 16)) }
    else if head.slice(32, 64) != shape { fail(OtherShape) }
    else { { is_base: read_u32(head, 12) & 1 == 1, before: read_u64(head, 24) } },
  }
}

// --- Hints ----------------------------------------------------------------------

fn hint_entry(key: Bytes, slot: Option<Slot>) -> Bytes =
  match slot {
    Some(s) ->
      Bytes::concat_all([
        b"\x01",
        u64_le(s.version),
        u64_le(s.at),
        u32_le(s.len),
        s.checksum,
        u32_le(key.len()),
        key,
      ]),
    None -> Bytes::concat_all([b"\x02", b"\x00".repeat(28), u32_le(key.len()), key]),
  }

/// The hint for segment `number`, `size` bytes long, whose frames wrote `touched` in records of
/// `weight` bytes and left the index in its current state.
fn hint_bytes(
  number: Int,
  size: Int,
  sequence: Int,
  weight: Int,
  index: Map<Bytes, Slot>,
  touched: Map<Bytes, Bool>,
) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} = {
  let entries = touched.fold([], |out: List<Bytes>, key: Bytes, put: Bool|
    out.push(hint_entry(key, if put { index.get(key) } else { None })));
  let said = Bytes::concat_all([
    hint_magic(),
    u32_le(format_version()),
    u32_le(0),
    u64_le(number),
    u64_le(size),
    u64_le(sequence),
    u64_le(entries.len()),
    u64_le(weight),
    Bytes::concat_all(entries),
  ]);
  said.concat(checksum(said))
}

/// `made` updated with segment `number`, `size` bytes long, as `hint` describes it. `None` if the
/// bytes are not a valid hint for that segment in its current state, in which case the segment is
/// read frame by frame.
fn apply_hint(
  state: ReadState,
  hint: Bytes,
  number: Int,
  size: Int,
) -> Option<ReadState> / {abort.raise} = {
  let length = hint.len();
  if length < hint_header_size() + 8 || !hint.starts_with(hint_magic())
      || checksum(hint.slice(0, length - 8)) != hint.slice(length - 8, length)
      || read_u32(hint, 8) != format_version()
      || read_u64(hint, 16) != number
      || read_u64(hint, 24) != size { None } else {
    let count = read_u64(hint, 40);
    let read = iterate(
      { index: state.index, left: count, p: hint_header_size(), ok: true },
      count + 1,
      |r: { index: Map<Bytes, Slot>, left: Int, p: Int, ok: Bool }|
        if r.left == 0 { Stop(r) }
        else if r.p + 33 > length - 8 { Stop({ ..r, ok: false }) } else {
          let keyed = r.p + 33;
          let after = keyed + read_u32(hint, r.p + 29);
          let kind = hint.at(r.p);
          if after > length - 8 || after == keyed || (kind != 1 && kind != 2) {
            Stop({ ..r, ok: false })
          } else {
            let key = hint.slice(keyed, after);
            Continue({
              index: if kind == 2 { r.index.remove(key) } else {
                r.index.insert(key, {
                  version: read_u64(hint, r.p + 1),
                  segment: number,
                  at: read_u64(hint, r.p + 9),
                  len: read_u32(hint, r.p + 17),
                  checksum: hint.slice(r.p + 21, r.p + 29),
                })
              },
              left: r.left - 1,
              p: after,
              ok: true,
            })
          }
        },
    );
    if !read.ok || read.p != length - 8 { None } else {
      Some({
        index: read.index,
        sequence: read_u64(hint, 32),
        touched: Map::new(),
        weight: state.weight + read_u64(hint, 48),
      })
    }
  }
}

// --- The lock ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Takes the directory's lock, trying `tries` times, and writes a new token into it.
fn take_lock<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  tries: Int,
) -> String / {fs.lock[l], fs.unlock[l], fs.write_file[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let asks = max(tries, 1);
  let taken = iterate(1, asks + 1, |n: Int|
    if fs.lock[l](lock_path(dir)) { Stop(true) }
    else if n >= asks { Stop(false) }
    else { Continue(n + 1) });
  let token = token_hex(16);
  if !taken { fail(Locked) }
  else if fs.write_file[l](lock_path(dir), Bytes::of_string(token)) { token } else {
    fs.unlock[l](lock_path(dir));
    fail(Failed(lock_path(dir), fs::operation_failed("write_file")))
  }
}

/// What a lock file contains after its token: the active segment's number and where the log ends.
fn log_position_of(active: Int, end: Int) -> Bytes = u64_le(active).concat(u64_le(end))

/// Reads the lock back and writes it again with `next` as the position after its token, which
/// keeps the lock from ageing. Fails with `LockLost` if the token is no longer this store's, and
/// with `Stale` if the position is not `was`, writing nothing in either case.
fn renew_lock<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  token: String,
  was: Bytes,
  next: Bytes,
) -> Unit / {fs.read_file[l], fs.write_file[l], key_value.fail} = {
  let mine = Bytes::of_string(token);
  match fs.read_file[l](lock_path(dir)) {
    None -> fail(LockLost),
    Some(found) -> if !found.starts_with(mine) { fail(LockLost) }
    else if found != mine.concat(was) { fail(Stale) }
    else if fs.write_file[l](lock_path(dir), mine.concat(next)) { () }
    else { fail(Failed(lock_path(dir), fs::operation_failed("write_file"))) },
  }
}

/// The log position the lock should contain for the `Store` value `s`.
fn log_position(s: Store) -> Bytes = log_position_of(s.active, s.end)

// --- Opening ----------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Writes a file completely or not at all, reporting a file system failure as the store's own.
fn write_whole<[l]>(
  path: String,
  body: Bytes,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Unit / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let wrote: Result<Unit, (String, fs::FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
    fs::write_atomic[l](path, body, durability)
  };
  match wrote { Ok(_) -> (), Err((at, error_of)) -> fail(Failed(at, error_of)) }
}

/// Cuts the segment at `path` to its first `keep` bytes, by copying it next to itself and renaming
/// the copy over it, so a crash leaves it either unchanged or cut.
fn truncate<[l]>(
  path: String,
  keep: Int,
) -> Unit / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let wrote: Result<Unit, (String, fs::FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
    io::replacing[l](path, Durable, None, |sink: Sink<| {fs.write_chunk[l]}>|
      range(0, (keep + read_chunk_size() - 1) / read_chunk_size()).fold((), |_: Unit, i: Int|
        match fs.read_at[l](
            path,
            i * read_chunk_size(),
            min(read_chunk_size(), keep - i * read_chunk_size()),
          ) {
          None -> fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("read_at"))),
          Some(bytes) -> if (sink.push)(bytes) { () }
          else { fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("write_chunk"))) },
        }))
  };
  match wrote { Ok(_) -> (), Err((at, error_of)) -> fail(Failed(at, error_of)) }
}

/// The length of the file at `path`.
fn size_of<[l]>(path: String) -> Int / {fs.file_size[l], key_value.fail} =
  match fs.file_size[l](path) { Some(n) -> n, None -> fail(Missing(path)) }

/// `made` updated with the full segment `number`: through its hint if it has one, and otherwise
/// frame by frame. Reading frame by frame rejects any bytes that are not a frame and a base
/// (`kept`) that does not end properly, and writes the hint.
fn read_full_segment<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  number: Int,
  limits: Limits,
  state: ReadState,
  is_base: Bool,
) -> ReadState / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let name = log_name(number);
  let path = join(dir, name);
  let size = size_of[l](path);
  let hint_path = join(dir, hint_name(number));
  let hint = match fs.file_size[l](hint_path) {
    Some(n) -> if n > 0x200_0000 { None } else { fs.read_file[l](hint_path) },
    None -> None,
  };
  let through = match hint { Some(bytes) -> apply_hint(state, bytes, number, size), None -> None };
  match through {
    Some(read) -> read,
    None -> {
      let w = scan_segment[l](
        path,
        name,
        number,
        size,
        limits,
        { ..state, touched: Map::new() },
        is_base,
      );
      if w.bad || (is_base && !w.closed) { fail(Corrupt(name, w.at)) } else {
        write_whole[l](
          hint_path,
          hint_bytes(
            number,
            size,
            w.made.sequence,
            w.made.weight - state.weight,
            w.made.index,
            w.made.touched,
          ),
          Fast,
        );
        w.made
      }
    },
  }
}

/// The store that a directory's files make up, under a lock already taken.
fn recover<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  token: String,
  limits: Limits,
  policy: Compaction,
  shape: Bytes,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let names = match fs::listed_names(fs.list_dir[l](dir)) {
    Some(found) -> found,
    None -> fail(Failed(dir, fs::operation_failed("list_dir"))),
  };
  names.fold((), |_: Unit, name: String|
    if name.starts_with(".tmp-") {
      fs.remove[l](join(dir, name));
      ()
    } else { () });
  let logs = names.fold([], |found: List<Int>, name: String|
    match file_number(name, ".log") { Some(n) -> found.push(n), None -> found }).sort(
  );
  let empty: Store = {
    dir: dir,
    token: token,
    limits: limits,
    policy: policy,
    shape: shape,
    index: Map::new(),
    sequence: 0,
    base: 1,
    active: 1,
    end: header_size(),
    synced_bodies: header_size(),
    total: 0,
    live: 0,
    weight: 0,
    touched: Map::new(),
    replaced: [],
  };
  let store = match logs.at(logs.len() - 1) {
    None -> {
      write_whole[l](join(dir, log_name(1)), header_bytes(1, false, 0, shape), Durable);
      empty
    },
    Some(top) -> {
      let lowest = logs.at(0).expect("the store has no log segments, so there is no lowest segment");
      let base = iterate(top, top + 1, |n: Int|
        if header_of[l](dir, n, shape).is_base || n == 1 { Stop(n) }
        else if n > lowest { Continue(n - 1) }
        else { fail(Missing(log_name(n - 1))) });
      names.fold((), |_: Unit, name: String| {
        let old = match file_number(name, ".log") { Some(n) -> n < base, None -> false };
        let stale = match file_number(name, ".hint") { Some(n) -> n < base, None -> false };
        if old || stale {
          fs.remove[l](join(dir, name));
          ()
        } else { () }
      });
      let based = header_of[l](dir, base, shape);
      let start: ReadState = {
        index: Map::new(),
        sequence: based.before,
        touched: Map::new(),
        weight: 0,
      };
      let whole = based.is_base && top == base;
      let segment_full = range(base, if whole { top + 1 } else { top }).fold(
        start,
        |state: ReadState, n: Int|
          if n > base && header_of[l](dir, n, shape).before != state.sequence {
            fail(Corrupt(log_name(n), 24))
          } else {
            renew_lock[l](dir, token, b"", b"");
            read_full_segment[l](dir, n, limits, state, n == base && based.is_base)
          },
      );
      if whole {
        write_whole[l](
          join(dir, log_name(top + 1)),
          header_bytes(top + 1, false, segment_full.sequence, shape),
          Durable,
        );
        {
          ..empty,
          index: segment_full.index,
          sequence: segment_full.sequence,
          base: base,
          active: top + 1,
          total: segment_full.weight,
          live: live_of(segment_full.index),
        }
      } else {
        let name = log_name(top);
        let path = join(dir, name);
        if top > base && header_of[l](dir, top, shape).before != segment_full.sequence {
          fail(Corrupt(name, 24))
        } else { () };
        let size = size_of[l](path);
        let w = scan_segment[l](
          path,
          name,
          top,
          size,
          limits,
          { ..segment_full, touched: Map::new() },
          false,
        );
        if w.bad {
          if has_synced_frame_after(read_range[l](path, w.at, size), w.at, limits) {
            fail(Corrupt(name, w.at))
          } else { truncate[l](path, w.at) }
        } else if fs.sync[l](path) { () }
        else { fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("sync"))) };
        {
          ..empty,
          index: w.made.index,
          sequence: w.made.sequence,
          base: base,
          active: top,
          end: w.at,
          synced_bodies: w.at,
          total: w.made.weight,
          live: live_of(w.made.index),
          weight: w.made.weight - segment_full.weight,
          touched: w.made.touched,
        }
      }
    },
  };
  renew_lock[l](dir, token, b"", log_position(store));
  store
}

/// The size of the records an index refers to.
fn live_of(index: Map<Bytes, Slot>) -> Int =
  index.fold(0, |bytes: Int, key: Bytes, slot: Slot| bytes + record_size(key, slot.len))

/// Opens the store in the directory `dir` under the root `l`, creating the directory and an empty
/// store if there is none, and returns it with the directory's lock taken. Use it as a `with_scope`
/// acquire function, with `close` to release. The lock is requested `tries` times, each waiting on
/// a holder as `fs.lock` does, and the result is `Locked` if another store still has it. A holder
/// that died leaves its lock behind, which can be taken once it is thirty seconds old: sixteen
/// tries wait that long. `shape` is `raw()` for a store of raw bytes, or `shape_of` the codecs it
/// is read with. A store written under another shape fails with `OtherShape`, and none of it is
/// read.
///
/// The result contains what the files contain: every batch whose frame is complete, in order, up
/// to the first that is not. A failure releases the lock.
pub fn open<[l]>(
  dir: String,
  limits: Limits,
  policy: Compaction,
  shape: Bytes,
  tries: Int,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  if limits.key < 1 || limits.value < 0 || limits.batch < limits.key + limits.value
      || limits.batch > 0x400_0000
      || limits.file < 1
      || shape.len() != 32 {
    panic(
      "invalid store limits or shape: `limits.key` must be at least 1 byte, `limits.batch` must be at least `limits.key + limits.value` and at most 64 MiB, `limits.file` must be at least 1 byte, and `shape` must be 32 bytes long",
    )
  } else if !fs.create_dir[l](dir) { fail(Failed(dir, fs::operation_failed("create_dir"))) } else {
    let token = take_lock[l](dir, tries);
    let read: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
      recover[l](dir, token, limits, policy, shape)
    };
    match read {
      Ok(store) -> store,
      Err(error_of) -> {
        fs.unlock[l](lock_path(dir));
        fail(error_of)
      },
    }
  }

/// Releases the lock `open` took, if it still belongs to this store.
pub fn close<[l]>(s: Store) -> Unit / {fs.read_file[l], fs.unlock[l]} =
  match fs.read_file[l](lock_path(s.dir)) {
    Some(found) -> if found.starts_with(Bytes::of_string(s.token)) {
      fs.unlock[l](lock_path(s.dir));
      ()
    } else { () },
    None -> (),
  }

// --- Reading ----------------------------------------------------------------------

fn value_at<[l]>(dir: String, slot: Slot) -> Bytes / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} = {
  let name = log_name(slot.segment);
  match fs.read_at[l](join(dir, name), slot.at, slot.len) {
    None -> fail(Missing(name)),
    Some(bytes) -> if bytes.len() != slot.len || checksum(bytes) != slot.checksum {
      fail(Corrupt(name, slot.at))
    } else { bytes },
  }
}

/// The store's current contents, as a value: reads through it see no later commit. It reads the
/// files its store had, so it stays valid while they exist, which is until the store `discard`s
/// what a compaction replaced or is opened again.
pub fn snapshot(s: Store) -> Snapshot = { dir: s.dir, index: s.index }

/// The value under `key` in the snapshot, read from its segment and checked against its checksum.
pub fn read<[l]>(
  snap: Snapshot,
  key: Bytes,
) -> Option<Bytes> / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  match snap.index.get(key) { Some(slot) -> Some(value_at[l](snap.dir, slot)), None -> None }

/// The value under `key`.
pub fn get<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  key: Bytes,
) -> Option<Bytes> / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} = read[l](snapshot(s), key)

/// The version of `key`: the sequence number of the batch that last wrote it, which a compaction
/// preserves. `None` for a key the store does not contain.
pub fn version(s: Store, key: Bytes) -> Option<Int> =
  match s.index.get(key) { Some(slot) -> Some(slot.version), None -> None }

/// How many keys the snapshot contains.
pub fn count(snap: Snapshot) -> Int = snap.index.len()

fn bounded(
  found: Option<{ key: Bytes, value: Slot }>,
  until: Option<Bytes>,
) -> Option<{ key: Bytes, value: Slot }> =
  match found {
    None -> None,
    Some(entry) -> match until {
      Some(stop) -> if compare_values(entry.key, stop) == Less { found } else { None },
      None -> found,
    },
  }

fn first_from(snap: Snapshot, from: Option<Bytes>) -> Option<{ key: Bytes, value: Slot }> =
  match from { Some(key) -> snap.index.ceiling(key), None -> snap.index.first() }

/// The snapshot's keys from `from` up to but not including `until`, in byte order, with no bound
/// at an end that is `None`. Nothing is read from disk: a pull is one step in the index.
pub fn keys(snap: Snapshot, from: Option<Bytes>, until: Option<Bytes>) -> Seq<Bytes | {}> =
  seq::unfold(bounded(first_from(snap, from), until), |next: Option<{ key: Bytes, value: Slot }>|
    match next {
      None -> None,
      Some(entry) -> Some({ value: entry.key, next: bounded(snap.index.above(entry.key), until) }),
    })

/// The snapshot's entries with keys from `from` up to but not including `until`, in byte order of
/// their keys, with no bound at an end that is `None`. A pull reads one value, so a consumer's
/// budget limits what a scan reads. A scan in progress is not affected by commits made while it is
/// pulled.
pub fn scan<[l]>(
  snap: Snapshot,
  from: Option<Bytes>,
  until: Option<Bytes>,
) -> Seq<Pair | {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise}> =
  seq::unfold(bounded(first_from(snap, from), until), |next: Option<{ key: Bytes, value: Slot }>|
    match next {
      None -> None,
      Some(entry) ->
        Some({
          value: {
            key: entry.key,
            value: value_at[l](snap.dir, entry.value),
            version: entry.value.version,
          },
          next: bounded(snap.index.above(entry.key), until),
        }),
    })

/// The smallest bytes greater than every key that begins with `prefix`. `None` if there are none,
/// which is when `prefix` is empty or all `FF`.
fn prefix_end(prefix: Bytes) -> Option<Bytes> / {abort.raise} =
  iterate(prefix.len(), prefix.len() + 1, |n: Int|
    if n == 0 { Stop(None) }
    else if prefix.at(n - 1) == 255 { Continue(n - 1) }
    else { Stop(Some(prefix.slice(0, n - 1).concat(byte_of_int(prefix.at(n - 1) + 1)))) })

/// The snapshot's entries whose keys begin with `prefix`, as `scan` returns them.
pub fn prefixed<[l]>(
  snap: Snapshot,
  prefix: Bytes,
) -> Seq<Pair | {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise}> / {abort.raise} =
  scan[l](snap, Some(prefix), prefix_end(prefix))

/// A summary of what the store contains.
pub fn stats(s: Store) -> Stats =
  {
    entries: s.index.len(),
    total: s.total,
    live: s.live,
    segments: s.active - s.base + 1,
    sequence: s.sequence,
  }

// --- Committing -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The total size of the keys and values in a batch, with each op checked against the store's
/// limits.
fn batch_weight(limits: Limits, ops: List<Op>) -> Int / {key_value.fail} =
  ops.fold(0, |bytes: Int, op: Op| {
    let key = match op { Put(k, _) -> k, Delete(k) -> k };
    let value = match op { Put(_, v) -> v.len(), Delete(_) -> 0 };
    if key.len() < 1 || key.len() > limits.key { fail(Unfit(Key)) }
    else if value > limits.value { fail(Unfit(Value)) }
    else { bytes + key.len() + value }
  })

/// Whether a commit through `s` begins a segment: its active one is full.
fn segment_full(s: Store) -> Bool = s.end >= s.limits.file

/// The path of segment `number` of `s`, which is about to be started. Fails with `Stale` if it
/// already exists, because then another writer started it, and its contents must not be
/// overwritten.
fn new_segment_path<[l]>(s: Store, number: Int) -> String / {fs.exists[l], key_value.fail} = {
  let path = join(s.dir, log_name(number));
  if fs.exists[l](path) { fail(Stale) } else { path }
}

/// If the active segment is full, the store with that segment closed and the next one started.
/// The full segment is synced and given its hint before the next segment exists to show that it
/// is full.
fn rotate_segment<[l]>(
  s: Store,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  if !segment_full(s) { s } else {
    let next = s.active + 1;
    let begun = new_segment_path[l](s, next);
    let path = join(s.dir, log_name(s.active));
    if fs.sync[l](path) { () } else { fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("sync"))) };
    write_whole[l](
      join(s.dir, hint_name(s.active)),
      hint_bytes(s.active, s.end, s.sequence, s.weight, s.index, s.touched),
      Fast,
    );
    write_whole[l](begun, header_bytes(next, false, s.sequence, s.shape), Durable);
    {
      ..s,
      active: next,
      end: header_size(),
      synced_bodies: header_size(),
      weight: 0,
      touched: Map::new(),
    }
  }

/// Commits `ops` as one batch. Once it returns, all of them are in the store, in order, so a key
/// the batch names twice has the value its last op left. If it raises before the frame is
/// appended, none of them are. The batch takes the next sequence number, which becomes the version
/// of each key it puts. An empty batch commits nothing. `Durable` syncs the log before returning;
/// `Fast` does not. A store whose policy requires it compacts before returning.
///
/// It raises `Unfit` for an op or a batch past the store's limits, and `LockLost` and `Stale` as
/// the module documentation describes, each with nothing committed. It raises `Failed` when the
/// file system fails. After a `Failed`, the batch may or may not be in the log. A later commit
/// through the same `Store` value succeeds only if the failed one wrote nothing, and fails with
/// `Stale` otherwise. Open the store again to read what the log contains.
pub fn batch<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  ops: List<Op>,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  if ops.len() == 0 { s }
  else if batch_weight(s.limits, ops) > s.limits.batch { fail(Unfit(Batch)) } else {
    let at = if segment_full(s) { header_size() } else { s.end };
    let body = body_of(s.sequence + 1, ops.map(op_bytes));
    let bytes = frame(at, if segment_full(s) { header_size() } else { s.synced_bodies }, body);
    let end = at + bytes.len();
    renew_lock[l](
      s.dir,
      s.token,
      log_position(s),
      log_position_of(if segment_full(s) { s.active + 1 } else { s.active }, end),
    );
    let r = rotate_segment[l](s);
    let name = log_name(r.active);
    let path = join(r.dir, name);
    match fs.append[l](path, bytes) {
      None -> fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("append"))),
      Some(landed) -> if landed != at { fail(Stale) }
      else if durability == Durable && !fs.sync[l](path) {
        fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("sync")))
      } else {
        let state = apply_frame(
          { index: r.index, sequence: r.sequence, touched: r.touched, weight: 0 },
          name,
          r.active,
          at + head_size(),
          body,
          false,
        );
        compact_if_due[l]({
          ..r,
          index: state.index,
          sequence: state.sequence,
          touched: state.touched,
          end: end,
          synced_bodies: if durability == Durable { end } else { r.synced_bodies },
          total: r.total + state.weight,
          live: r.live + live_weight(state.index, ops) - live_weight(r.index, ops),
          weight: r.weight + state.weight,
        })
      },
    }
  }

/// The size of the records the index has for the keys `ops` names, counting each key once.
fn live_weight(index: Map<Bytes, Slot>, ops: List<Op>) -> Int =
  ops.fold(Map::new(), |seen: Map<Bytes, Unit>, op: Op|
    seen.insert(match op { Put(k, _) -> k, Delete(k) -> k }, ())).fold(
    0,
    |bytes: Int, key: Bytes, _: Unit|
      match index.get(key) { Some(slot) -> bytes + record_size(key, slot.len), None -> bytes },
  )

/// The store, compacted if its policy says enough of its log is dead.
fn compact_if_due<[l]>(
  s: Store,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  match s.policy {
    Manual -> s,
    WhenDead(percent, at_least) -> {
      let dead = s.total - s.live;
      if dead > 0 && dead >= at_least && dead * 100 >= s.total * percent { rewrite_as_base[l](s) }
      else { s }
    },
  }

/// Puts `value` under `key`: a batch of one.
pub fn put<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  key: Bytes,
  value: Bytes,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  batch[l](s, [Put(key, value)], durability)

/// Deletes `key`, which need not be there: a batch of one.
pub fn delete<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  key: Bytes,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  batch[l](s, [Delete(key)], durability)

/// Puts `value` under `key`, or deletes the key if `value` is `None`, only if the key's version is
/// `expected`, where `None` means the store does not contain the key. `done` reports whether the
/// version matched. If it did not, nothing is committed, and the caller should read again and
/// decide again.
pub fn compare_and_set<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  key: Bytes,
  expected: Option<Int>,
  value: Option<Bytes>,
  durability: Durability,
) -> CompareAndSetResult / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  if version(s, key) != expected { { store: s, done: false } } else {
    {
      store: batch[l](
        s,
        [match value { Some(bytes) -> Put(key, bytes), None -> Delete(key) }],
        durability,
      ),
      done: true,
    }
  }

// --- Compacting -------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A compaction in progress: the kept records waiting for a frame and the size of their keys and
/// values, where each one's value is within its record, the offset to write the next frame at, the
/// index of what is written, and how much has been copied since the lock was renewed.
type CompactionState = {
  waiting: List<Bytes>,
  weight: Int,
  places: List<{ key: Bytes, version: Int, within: Int, len: Int, sum: Bytes }>,
  size: Int,
  at: Int,
  index: Map<Bytes, Slot>,
  since_renewal: Int,
}

/// How much a compaction copies between renewals of its lock, where each value counts as one plus
/// one for each 16 KiB of it.
fn renew_interval() -> Int = 64

/// Writes the waiting records as a frame into the sink, and updates the index with their values at
/// their new offsets.
fn flush_frame<[l]>(
  p: CompactionState,
  number: Int,
  path: String,
  sink: Sink<| {fs.write_chunk[l]}>,
) -> CompactionState / {fs.write_chunk[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  if p.waiting.len() == 0 { p } else {
    let bytes = frame(p.at, header_size(), body_of(0, p.waiting));
    if !(sink.push)(bytes) { fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("write_chunk"))) } else {
      {
        waiting: [],
        weight: 0,
        places: [],
        size: body_head_size(),
        at: p.at + bytes.len(),
        index: p.places.fold(
          p.index,
          |index: Map<Bytes, Slot>, log_position: { key: Bytes, version: Int, within: Int, len: Int, sum: Bytes }|
            index.insert(log_position.key, {
              version: log_position.version,
              segment: number,
              at: p.at + head_size() + log_position.within,
              len: log_position.len,
              checksum: log_position.sum,
            }),
        ),
        since_renewal: p.since_renewal,
      }
    }
  }

/// The store with every value it contains written into one new base segment, which is renamed into
/// place whole, followed by a new active segment. The lock records the log position after the
/// compaction before the base gets its name, so no `Store` value from before the compaction can
/// commit afterwards.
fn rewrite_as_base<[l]>(
  s: Store,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  let number = s.active + 1;
  let path = new_segment_path[l](s, number);
  let here = log_position(s);
  let wrote: Result<CompactionState, (String, fs::FileError)> = try[files.fail] {
    io::replacing[l](path, Durable, None, |sink: Sink<| {fs.write_chunk[l]}>| {
      let said = |bytes: Bytes|
        if (sink.push)(bytes) { () }
        else { fail(Failed(path, fs::operation_failed("write_chunk"))) };
      said(header_bytes(number, true, s.sequence, s.shape));
      let copied = s.index.fold(
        {
          waiting: [],
          weight: 0,
          places: [],
          size: body_head_size(),
          at: header_size(),
          index: Map::new(),
          since_renewal: 0,
        },
        |p: CompactionState, key: Bytes, slot: Slot| {
          let value = value_at[l](s.dir, slot);
          let weight = key.len() + slot.len;
          let room = if p.weight > 0 && p.weight + weight > s.limits.batch {
            flush_frame[l](p, number, path, sink)
          } else { p };
          let due = room.since_renewal >= renew_interval();
          if due { renew_lock[l](s.dir, s.token, here, here) } else { () };
          {
            ..room,
            waiting: room.waiting.push(record(3, key, u64_le(slot.version), value)),
            weight: room.weight + weight,
            places: room.places.push({
              key: key,
              version: slot.version,
              within: room.size + 17 + key.len(),
              len: slot.len,
              sum: slot.checksum,
            }),
            size: room.size + 17 + weight,
            since_renewal: (if due { 0 } else { room.since_renewal }) + 1 + slot.len / 0x4000,
          }
        },
      );
      let done = flush_frame[l](copied, number, path, sink);
      let last = frame(done.at, header_size(), body_of(0, []));
      said(last);
      renew_lock[l](s.dir, s.token, here, log_position_of(number + 1, header_size()));
      { ..done, at: done.at + last.len() }
    })
  };
  match wrote {
    Err((at, error_of)) -> fail(Failed(at, error_of)),
    Ok(done) -> {
      let all = done.index.fold(Map::new(), |m: Map<Bytes, Bool>, key: Bytes, _: Slot|
        m.insert(key, true));
      write_whole[l](
        join(s.dir, hint_name(number)),
        hint_bytes(number, done.at, s.sequence, s.live, done.index, all),
        Fast,
      );
      let next = number + 1;
      write_whole[l](
        join(s.dir, log_name(next)),
        header_bytes(next, false, s.sequence, s.shape),
        Durable,
      );
      {
        ..s,
        index: done.index,
        base: number,
        active: next,
        end: header_size(),
        synced_bodies: header_size(),
        total: s.live,
        weight: 0,
        touched: Map::new(),
        replaced: range(s.base, number).fold(s.replaced, |old: List<Int>, n: Int| old.push(n)),
      }
    },
  }
}

/// Writes every value the store contains into one new base segment, and renames it into place.
/// Space taken by deleted and overwritten records is no longer read, and a store opened after a
/// crash at any step finds either the old segments or the base, never neither. Versions are
/// preserved. The lock is renewed as the values are copied, so a compaction of any length is not
/// mistaken for a dead store. The replaced segments stay on disk for snapshots taken before the
/// compaction, until `discard` or the next `open`.
pub fn compact<[l]>(
  s: Store,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} = {
  renew_lock[l](s.dir, s.token, log_position(s), log_position(s));
  rewrite_as_base[l](s)
}

/// Removes the segments compactions replaced. A snapshot taken before the compaction that
/// replaced one can no longer read it, and raises `Missing` where it tries.
pub fn discard<[l]>(
  s: Store,
) -> Store / {fs.read_file[l], fs.write_file[l], fs.remove[l], key_value.fail} = {
  renew_lock[l](s.dir, s.token, log_position(s), log_position(s));
  s.replaced.fold((), |_: Unit, n: Int| {
    fs.remove[l](join(s.dir, log_name(n)));
    fs.remove[l](join(s.dir, hint_name(n)));
    ()
  });
  { ..s, replaced: [] }
}

// --- Values of a type -------------------------------------------------------------

/// The codecs a store's keys and values are written with.
pub type Codecs<k, v> = { key: BinCodec<k>, value: BinCodec<v> }

/// The shape to open a store with to read it through `codecs`: a digest of both types as
/// `bin::shape_digest` computes it. So `open` rejects a store written before a type changed, and
/// none of its values is read as the new type.
pub fn shape_of<k, v>(codecs: Codecs<k, v>) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  bin::shape_digest(codecs.key).concat(bin::shape_digest(codecs.value)).blake3()

/// `key` as the store stores it. Keys are ordered by these bytes, which matches the order of the
/// key values only if the codec's encoding preserves order.
pub fn key_bytes<k, v>(codecs: Codecs<k, v>, key: k) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  bin::encode(key, codecs.key)

/// The op that puts `value` under `key`, for a batch.
pub fn put_op<k, v>(codecs: Codecs<k, v>, key: k, value: v) -> Op / {abort.raise} =
  Put(bin::encode(key, codecs.key), bin::encode(value, codecs.value))

/// A scan's entry decoded to the types it was written from. Fails with `Undecodable` if its bytes
/// are not what the codecs write.
pub fn typed<k, v>(
  codecs: Codecs<k, v>,
  pair: Pair,
) -> { key: k, value: v, version: Int } / {key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  match bin::decode(pair.key, codecs.key) {
    Err(e) -> fail(Undecodable(bin::error_to_string(e))),
    Ok(key) -> match bin::decode(pair.value, codecs.value) {
      Err(e) -> fail(Undecodable(bin::error_to_string(e))),
      Ok(value) -> { key: key, value: value, version: pair.version },
    },
  }

/// Puts `value` under `key`, each written with its codec.
pub fn put_as<k, v, [l]>(
  s: Store,
  codecs: Codecs<k, v>,
  key: k,
  value: v,
  durability: Durability,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  batch[l](s, [put_op(codecs, key, value)], durability)

/// The value under `key`, read with its codec. Fails with `Undecodable` if the bytes are not a
/// value of the type.
pub fn get_as<k, v, [l]>(
  s: Store,
  codecs: Codecs<k, v>,
  key: k,
) -> Option<v> / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  match get[l](s, key_bytes(codecs, key)) {
    None -> None,
    Some(bytes) -> match bin::decode(bytes, codecs.value) {
      Ok(value) -> Some(value),
      Err(e) -> fail(Undecodable(bin::error_to_string(e))),
    },
  }

// --- Tests ------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Limits under which the third commit of a short value begins a segment.
fn small() -> Limits = { key: 8, value: 64, batch: 96, file: 160 }

/// Limits under which nothing a test here commits fills a segment.
fn large_limits() -> Limits = { key: 8, value: 64, batch: 4096, file: 0x10_0000 }

/// What `go` returns for the store in `db` of `tree`, opened with entropy from `seed` and closed
/// afterwards, or why the store failed, along with the resulting tree.
fn opened<a>(
  tree: MemFs,
  seed: Int,
  limits: Limits,
  policy: Compaction,
  shape: Bytes,
  go: (Store) -> a / {fs.read[d], fs.write[d], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise},
) -> MemResult<Result<a, Error>> / {abort.raise} =
  on_mem[d](tree, ||
    with_rand(rand(seed), || {
      let ran: Result<a, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
        with_scope[store](open[d]("db", limits, policy, shape, 1), |s: Store|
          close[d](s)) { held -> go(held.get()) }
      };
      ran
    }).value)

/// `opened` for a store of raw bytes that compacts only on request.
fn session<a>(
  tree: MemFs,
  seed: Int,
  limits: Limits,
  go: (Store) -> a / {fs.read[d], fs.write[d], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise},
) -> MemResult<Result<a, Error>> / {abort.raise} = opened(tree, seed, limits, Manual, raw(), go)

fn listed<a | e>(found: Seq<a | e>) -> List<a> / {abort.raise | e} = {
  let all: Result<List<a>, Int> = try[seq.spent] { seq::to_list(found, 100000) };
  match all { Ok(list) -> list, Err(_) -> panic("the sequence has more than 100000 items, which is more than a test can read") }
}

fn error_of<a>(r: Result<a, Error>) -> Option<Error> = match r { Ok(_) -> None, Err(w) -> Some(w) }

/// A store's contents in the form a map gives them: its entries in key order, and how many batches
/// it took.
type Shown = { pairs: List<Pair>, sequence: Int }

fn shown<[l]>(s: Store) -> Shown / {fs.read_at[l], key_value.fail, abort.raise} =
  { pairs: listed(scan[l](snapshot(s), None, None)), sequence: stats(s).sequence }

/// What a map containing `m` contains after one batch.
fn stepped(m: Shown, ops: List<Op>) -> Shown =
  if ops.len() == 0 { m } else {
    let version = m.sequence + 1;
    let before = m.pairs.fold(Map::new(), |held: Map<Bytes, Pair>, p: Pair| held.insert(p.key, p));
    let after = ops.fold(before, |held: Map<Bytes, Pair>, op: Op|
      match op {
        Put(key, value) -> held.insert(key, { key: key, value: value, version: version }),
        Delete(key) -> held.remove(key),
      });
    { pairs: after.values(), sequence: version }
  }

test "a put is read back under its batch's number, a delete removes it, and an older store value reads what it had" {
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"one", Fast);
    let s2 = batch[d](s1, [Put(b"b", b"two"), Put(b"a", b"uno"), Delete(b"c")], Durable);
    let s3 = delete[d](s2, b"b", Fast);
    {
      values: [
        get[d](s0, b"a"),
        get[d](s1, b"a"),
        get[d](s2, b"a"),
        get[d](s2, b"b"),
        get[d](s3, b"b"),
      ],
      versions: [
        version(s0, b"a"),
        version(s1, b"a"),
        version(s2, b"a"),
        version(s2, b"b"),
        version(s3, b"b"),
      ],
      held: count(snapshot(s2)),
      after: shown[d](s3),
      batches: stats(batch[d](s3, [], Durable)).sequence,
    }
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      values: [None, Some(b"one"), Some(b"uno"), Some(b"two"), None],
      versions: [None, Some(1), Some(2), Some(2), None],
      held: 2,
      after: { pairs: [{ key: b"a", value: b"uno", version: 2 }], sequence: 3 },
      batches: 3,
    }),
  )
}

test "a reopened store has what was committed, each key under its version, and numbers its next batch after the last" {
  let first = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = batch[d](s0, [Put(b"a", b"1"), Put(b"b", b"2")], Fast);
    shown[d](delete[d](put[d](s1, b"c", b"3", Durable), b"a", Fast))
  });
  let held = {
    pairs: [{ key: b"b", value: b"2", version: 1 }, { key: b"c", value: b"3", version: 2 }],
    sequence: 3,
  };
  assert_eq(first.value, Ok(held));
  let second = session(first.tree, 2, small(), |s: Store|
    (shown[d](s), version(put[d](s, b"d", b"4", Fast), b"d")));
  assert_eq(second.value, Ok((held, Some(4))))
}

/// A frame as the format states it, from its offset, its length, its mark and its body.
fn expected_frame(at: Int, mark: Int, body: Bytes) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} = {
  let length = u32_le(body.len());
  let sum = Bytes::concat_all([u64_le(at), length, u64_le(mark), body]).blake3();
  Bytes::concat_all([b"\xf1kv\n", length, sum.slice(0, 8), u64_le(mark), body])
}

test "a store's files follow the format its module documents" {
  let header = |flags: Int, number: Int, before: Int|
    Bytes::concat_all([
      b"PLYKVLOG",
      u32_le(1),
      u32_le(flags),
      u64_le(number),
      u64_le(before),
      b"\x00".repeat(32),
    ]);
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"k", b"v", Durable);
    let lock = fs.read_file[d]("db/lock");
    let log = fs.read_file[d]("db/00000001.log");
    compact[d](s1);
    { lock: lock, table_log: log }
  });
  let batch_body = Bytes::concat_all([
    u64_le(1),
    u32_le(1),
    b"\x01",
    u32_le(1),
    u32_le(1),
    b"k",
    b"v",
  ]);
  let log = header(0, 1, 0).concat(expected_frame(64, 64, batch_body));
  let found = match over.value { Ok(read) -> read, Err(_) -> panic("the store rejected") };
  assert_eq(found.table_log, Some(log));
  let lock = match found.lock { Some(bytes) -> bytes, None -> b"" };
  assert_eq(lock.len(), 48);
  assert_eq(lock.slice(32, 48), u64_le(1).concat(u64_le(log.len())));
  let kept_body = Bytes::concat_all([
    u64_le(0),
    u32_le(1),
    b"\x03",
    u32_le(1),
    u32_le(1),
    u64_le(1),
    b"k",
    b"v",
  ]);
  let base = Bytes::concat_all([
    header(1, 2, 1),
    expected_frame(64, 64, kept_body),
    expected_frame(119, 64, u64_le(0).concat(u32_le(0))),
  ]);
  let said = Bytes::concat_all([
    b"PLYKVHNT",
    u32_le(1),
    u32_le(0),
    u64_le(2),
    u64_le(base.len()),
    u64_le(1),
    u64_le(1),
    u64_le(19),
    b"\x01",
    u64_le(1),
    u64_le(118),
    u32_le(1),
    b"v".blake3().slice(0, 8),
    u32_le(1),
    b"k",
  ]);
  assert_eq(
    fs::listed_names(mem_list(over.tree, "db")),
    Some(["00000001.log", "00000002.hint", "00000002.log", "00000003.log"]),
  );
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "db/00000001.log"), Some(log));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "db/00000002.log"), Some(base));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "db/00000002.hint"), Some(said.concat(said.blake3().slice(0, 8))));
  assert_eq(mem_read(over.tree, "db/00000003.log"), Some(header(0, 3, 1)))
}

test "a key, a value or a batch past a store's limits is rejected, and nothing is written for it" {
  let tried = |ops: List<Op>|
    session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s: Store| {
      batch[d](s, ops, Fast);
      fs.file_size[d]("db/00000001.log")
    }).value;
  assert_eq(tried([Put(b"", b"v")]), Err(Unfit(Key)));
  assert_eq(tried([Put(b"123456789", b"v")]), Err(Unfit(Key)));
  assert_eq(tried([Delete(b"")]), Err(Unfit(Key)));
  assert_eq(tried([Put(b"k", b"v".repeat(65))]), Err(Unfit(Value)));
  assert_eq(tried([Put(b"a", b"v".repeat(60)), Put(b"b", b"v".repeat(60))]), Err(Unfit(Batch)));
  assert_eq(tried([Put(b"a", b"v".repeat(47)), Put(b"b", b"v".repeat(47))]), Ok(Some(214)));
  let unwritten = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s: Store| {
    let rejected: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
      put[d](s, b"k", b"v".repeat(65), Fast)
    };
    (error_of(rejected), fs.file_size[d]("db/00000001.log"))
  });
  assert_eq(unwritten.value, Ok((Some(Unfit(Value)), Some(64))))
}

/// What `go` returns, and how many ranged reads of the root it made.
fn counting<a, [l] | e>(
  go: () -> a / {fs.read_at[l] | e},
) -> { value: a, reads: Int } / {fs.read_at[l] | e} =
  handle {
    go()
  } with {
    var n = 0,
    fs.read_at[l](path, at, most) -> {
      n = n + 1;
      fs.read_at[l](path, at, most)
    },
    return value -> { value: value, reads: n },
  }

fn keys_of<| e>(found: Seq<Pair | e>) -> List<Bytes> / {abort.raise | e} =
  listed(found).map(|p: Pair| p.key)

test "a scan returns a range of keys in byte order, a prefix its keys, and reads no more than is pulled" {
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
    let s = [b"b/2", b"a", b"b/1", b"c", b"b", b"b/\xff"].fold(s0, |s: Store, key: Bytes|
      put[d](s, key, b"at ".concat(key), Fast));
    let snap = snapshot(s);
    let pulled = counting[d](|| {
      let two: Result<List<Pair>, Int> = try[seq.spent] {
        seq::to_list(seq::take(scan[d](snap, None, None), 2), 10)
      };
      two
    });
    let spent: Result<List<Pair>, Int> = try[seq.spent] {
      seq::to_list(scan[d](snap, None, None), 3)
    };
    {
      all: keys_of(scan[d](snap, None, None)),
      within: keys_of(scan[d](snap, Some(b"b"), Some(b"c"))),
      onward: keys_of(scan[d](snap, Some(b"b/"), None)),
      before: keys_of(scan[d](snap, None, Some(b"b"))),
      under: keys_of(prefixed[d](snap, b"b/")),
      every: keys_of(prefixed[d](snap, b"")).len(),
      last: keys_of(prefixed[d](snap, b"b/\xff")),
      none: keys_of(prefixed[d](snap, b"\xff")),
      names: counting[d](|| listed(keys(snap, Some(b"b/1"), Some(b"b/3")))),
      pulled: pulled,
      spent: spent,
    }
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      all: [b"a", b"b", b"b/1", b"b/2", b"b/\xff", b"c"],
      within: [b"b", b"b/1", b"b/2", b"b/\xff"],
      onward: [b"b/1", b"b/2", b"b/\xff", b"c"],
      before: [b"a"],
      under: [b"b/1", b"b/2", b"b/\xff"],
      every: 6,
      last: [b"b/\xff"],
      none: [],
      names: { value: [b"b/1", b"b/2"], reads: 0 },
      pulled: {
        value: Ok([
          { key: b"a", value: b"at a", version: 2 },
          { key: b"b", value: b"at b", version: 5 },
        ]),
        reads: 2,
      },
      spent: Err(3),
    }),
  )
}

test "a snapshot reads what its store had when it was taken, through later commits and a compaction, until its files are discarded" {
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = batch[d](s0, [Put(b"a", b"old a"), Put(b"b", b"old b")], Fast);
    let snap = snapshot(s1);
    let begun: Result<Option<{ value: Pair, rest: Seq<Pair | {fs.read_at[d], key_value.fail, abort.raise}> }>, Int> = try[seq.spent] {
      seq::pull(scan[d](snap, None, None), 4)
    };
    let s2 = batch[d](s1, [Put(b"a", b"new a"), Delete(b"b"), Put(b"c", b"new c")], Durable);
    let rest = match begun { Ok(Some(first)) -> keys_of(first.rest), _ -> [] };
    let packed = compact[d](s2);
    let through = listed(scan[d](snap, None, None));
    let cleared = discard[d](packed);
    let gone: Result<Option<Bytes>, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { read[d](snap, b"a") };
    { rest: rest, through: through, gone: gone, now: shown[d](cleared), held: count(snap) }
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      rest: [b"b"],
      through: [
        { key: b"a", value: b"old a", version: 1 },
        { key: b"b", value: b"old b", version: 1 },
      ],
      gone: Err(Missing("00000001.log")),
      now: {
        pairs: [
          { key: b"a", value: b"new a", version: 2 },
          { key: b"c", value: b"new c", version: 2 },
        ],
        sequence: 2,
      },
      held: 2,
    }),
  )
}

/// A store of six keys under `small`, which fills two segments and begins a third.
fn six_key_store() -> MemResult<Result<Shown, Error>> / {abort.raise} =
  session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store|
    shown[d](
      range(1, 7).fold(s0, |s: Store, i: Int|
        put[d](s, b"k".concat(byte_of_int(48 + i)), byte_of_int(48 + i).repeat(20), Fast)),
    ))

/// The tree with one byte of the file at `path` changed.
fn flip_byte(tree: MemFs, path: String, at: Int) -> MemFs / {abort.raise} = {
  let had = mem_read(tree, path).expect("the file to change does not exist");
  mem_write(
    tree,
    path,
    Bytes::concat_all([
      had.slice(0, at),
      byte_of_int(255 - had.at(at)),
      had.slice(at + 1, had.len()),
    ]),
  ).fs
}

/// The tree with the file at `path` cut to its first `keep` bytes.
fn shortened(tree: MemFs, path: String, keep: Int) -> MemFs / {abort.raise} =
  mem_write(tree, path, mem_read(tree, path).expect("the file to shorten does not exist").slice(0, keep)).fs

test "a full segment has a hint that opening reads instead, and a hint for another segment is ignored and written again" {
  let state = six_key_store();
  let hint = "db/00000001.hint";
  assert_eq(
    fs::listed_names(mem_list(state.tree, "db")),
    Some(["00000001.hint", "00000001.log", "00000002.hint", "00000002.log", "00000003.log"]),
  );
  let held = |tree: MemFs| session(tree, 2, small(), |s: Store| (shown[d](s), stats(s)));
  let whole = held(state.tree);
  assert_eq(whole.value, match state.value {
    Ok(all) -> Ok((all, { entries: 6, total: 234, live: 234, segments: 3, sequence: 6 })),
    Err(rejection) -> Err(rejection),
  });
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(state.tree, "db/00000001.log", 70)).value, whole.value);
  let unhinted = held(mem_remove(state.tree, hint).fs);
  assert_eq(unhinted.value, whole.value);
  assert_eq(mem_read(unhinted.tree, hint), mem_read(state.tree, hint));
  let misled = held(flip_byte(state.tree, hint, 60));
  assert_eq(misled.value, whole.value);
  assert_eq(mem_read(misled.tree, hint), mem_read(state.tree, hint));
  let rotten = flip_byte(state.tree, "db/00000001.log", 115);
  let read = session(rotten, 2, small(), |s: Store| {
    let lost: Result<Option<Bytes>, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { get[d](s, b"k1") };
    (lost, get[d](s, b"k2"))
  });
  assert_eq(read.value, Ok((Err(Corrupt("00000001.log", 111)), Some(b"2".repeat(20)))));
  assert_eq(held(mem_remove(rotten, hint).fs).value, Err(Corrupt("00000001.log", 64)))
}

test "a compaction keeps every value under its version in one base, and what it replaced stays until it is discarded" {
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let filled = range(0, 6).fold(s0, |s: Store, i: Int|
      put[d](s, if i % 2 == 0 { b"even" } else { b"odd" }, byte_of_int(48 + i).repeat(20), Fast));
    let dropped = delete[d](filled, b"odd", Fast);
    let before = (shown[d](dropped), stats(dropped));
    let packed = compact[d](dropped);
    let is_base = fs::listed_names(fs.list_dir[d]("db"));
    let cleared = discard[d](packed);
    {
      before: before,
      packed: (shown[d](packed), stats(packed)),
      kept: is_base,
      left: fs::listed_names(fs.list_dir[d]("db")),
      next: version(put[d](cleared, b"new", b"n", Durable), b"new"),
    }
  });
  let held = { pairs: [{ key: b"even", value: b"4".repeat(20), version: 5 }], sequence: 7 };
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      before: (held, { entries: 1, total: 263, live: 41, segments: 4, sequence: 7 }),
      packed: (held, { entries: 1, total: 41, live: 41, segments: 2, sequence: 7 }),
      kept: Some([
        "00000001.hint",
        "00000001.log",
        "00000002.hint",
        "00000002.log",
        "00000003.hint",
        "00000003.log",
        "00000004.log",
        "00000005.hint",
        "00000005.log",
        "00000006.log",
        "lock",
      ]),
      left: Some(["00000005.hint", "00000005.log", "00000006.log", "lock"]),
      next: Some(8),
    }),
  );
  let again = session(over.tree, 2, small(), |s: Store| (shown[d](s), stats(s)));
  assert_eq(
    again.value,
    Ok((
      stepped(held, [Put(b"new", b"n")]),
      { entries: 2, total: 62, live: 62, segments: 2, sequence: 8 },
    )),
  )
}

test "a store set to compact does so in the commit that leaves enough of its log dead, and not again until as much is" {
  let over = opened(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), WhenDead(50, 40), raw(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"k", b"1".repeat(20), Fast);
    let s2 = put[d](s1, b"k", b"2".repeat(20), Fast);
    let s3 = put[d](s2, b"k", b"3".repeat(20), Fast);
    let s4 = put[d](s3, b"j", b"", Fast);
    ([stats(s1), stats(s2), stats(s3), stats(s4)], shown[d](s4))
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok((
      [
        { entries: 1, total: 38, live: 38, segments: 1, sequence: 1 },
        { entries: 1, total: 76, live: 38, segments: 1, sequence: 2 },
        { entries: 1, total: 38, live: 38, segments: 2, sequence: 3 },
        { entries: 2, total: 56, live: 56, segments: 2, sequence: 4 },
      ],
      {
        pairs: [
          { key: b"j", value: b"", version: 4 },
          { key: b"k", value: b"3".repeat(20), version: 3 },
        ],
        sequence: 4,
      },
    )),
  )
}

test "a log that ends inside a frame opens to the batches before it, is cut to them, and takes the next commit" {
  let batches = [
    [Put(b"a", b"first")],
    [Put(b"b", b"second"), Delete(b"a")],
    [Put(b"c", b"third")],
  ];
  let log = "db/00000001.log";
  let state = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store|
    batches.fold(
      { store: s0, ends: [] },
      |at: { store: Store, ends: List<Int> }, ops: List<Op>| {
        let next = batch[d](at.store, ops, Fast);
        { store: next, ends: at.ends.push(fs.file_size[d](log).expect("the log")) }
      },
    ).ends);
  let ends = match state.value { Ok(found) -> found, Err(_) -> [] };
  assert_eq(ends, [115, 177, 228]);
  range(64, 229).fold((), |_: Unit, keep: Int| {
    let whole = ends.filter(|end: Int| end <= keep).len();
    let held = batches.take(whole).fold({ pairs: [], sequence: 0 }, stepped);
    let again = session(shortened(state.tree, log, keep), 2, large_limits(), |s: Store| {
      let found = (shown[d](s), fs.file_size[d](log));
      put[d](s, b"z", b"after", Durable);
      found
    });
    let truncate = match ends.at(whole - 1) { Some(end) -> end, None -> 64 };
    assert_eq(again.value, Ok((held, Some(truncate))));
    let after = session(again.tree, 3, large_limits(), |s: Store| shown[d](s));
    assert_eq(after.value, Ok(stepped(held, [Put(b"z", b"after")])))
  })
}

test "damage that a sync had covered is reported where it is, and what no sync covered is cut with all that follows it" {
  let log = "db/00000001.log";
  let state = |first: Durability, second: Durability|
    session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
      let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"first", first);
      put[d](put[d](s1, b"b", b"second", second), b"c", b"third", Fast);
      ()
    }).tree;
  let held = |tree: MemFs|
    session(tree, 2, large_limits(), |s: Store| listed(keys(snapshot(s), None, None))).value;
  let early = state(Durable, Fast);
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(early, log, 100)), Err(Corrupt("00000001.log", 64)));
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(early, log, 150)), Ok([b"a"]));
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(early, log, 200)), Ok([b"a", b"b"]));
  let late = state(Fast, Durable);
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(late, log, 100)), Err(Corrupt("00000001.log", 64)));
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(late, log, 150)), Err(Corrupt("00000001.log", 115)));
  assert_eq(held(flip_byte(late, log, 200)), Ok([b"a", b"b"]));
  let rejected = session(flip_byte(late, log, 100), 2, large_limits(), |s: Store| ());
  assert_eq(mem_read(rejected.tree, log), mem_read(flip_byte(late, log, 100), log));
  assert_eq(fs::listed_names(mem_list(rejected.tree, "db")), Some(["00000001.log"]))
}

test "a directory that is not a complete store is rejected with what is wrong with it" {
  let state = six_key_store().tree;
  let failure_of = |tree: MemFs, shape: Bytes|
    error_of(opened(tree, 2, small(), Manual, shape, |s: Store| ()).value);
  assert_eq(failure_of(state, raw()), None);
  assert_eq(failure_of(state, b"\x01".repeat(32)), Some(OtherShape));
  assert_eq(
    failure_of(flip_byte(state, "db/00000003.log", 0), raw()),
    Some(Corrupt("00000003.log", 0)),
  );
  assert_eq(failure_of(flip_byte(state, "db/00000003.log", 8), raw()), Some(OtherFormat(254)));
  assert_eq(
    failure_of(flip_byte(state, "db/00000003.log", 16), raw()),
    Some(Corrupt("00000003.log", 16)),
  );
  assert_eq(
    failure_of(mem_remove(state, "db/00000002.log").fs, raw()),
    Some(Missing("00000002.log")),
  );
  assert_eq(
    failure_of(mem_remove(state, "db/00000001.log").fs, raw()),
    Some(Missing("00000001.log")),
  );
  let lost = mem_remove(shortened(state, "db/00000001.log", 131), "db/00000001.hint").fs;
  assert_eq(failure_of(lost, raw()), Some(Corrupt("00000002.log", 24)));
  let packed = session(state, 2, small(), |s: Store| {
    compact[d](s);
    ()
  }).tree;
  assert_eq(failure_of(packed, raw()), None);
  let base = "db/00000004.log";
  let size = mem_size(packed, base).expect("the base");
  let truncate = mem_remove(shortened(packed, base, size - 36), "db/00000004.hint").fs;
  assert_eq(failure_of(truncate, raw()), Some(Corrupt("00000004.log", size - 36)));
  assert_eq(
    failure_of(mem_remove(flip_byte(packed, base, size - 40), "db/00000004.hint").fs, raw()),
    Some(Corrupt("00000004.log", 256)),
  )
}

test "one store has a directory at a time, and a value of a store that is closed, or behind another, commits nothing" {
  let log = "db/00000001.log";
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
    let second: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
      open[d]("db", large_limits(), Manual, raw(), 3)
    };
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"1", Fast);
    let size = fs.file_size[d](log);
    let behind: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](s0, b"b", b"2", Fast) };
    let unwritten = fs.file_size[d](log) == size;
    let s2 = put[d](s1, b"c", b"3", Fast);
    let packed = compact[d](s2);
    let late: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](s2, b"d", b"4", Fast) };
    let again: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { compact[d](s2) };
    let cleared: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { discard[d](s2) };
    {
      rejections: [
        error_of(second),
        error_of(behind),
        error_of(late),
        error_of(again),
        error_of(cleared),
      ],
      unwritten: unwritten,
      held: keys_of(scan[d](snapshot(put[d](packed, b"e", b"5", Fast)), None, None)),
    }
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      rejections: [Some(Locked), Some(Stale), Some(Stale), Some(Stale), Some(Stale)],
      unwritten: true,
      held: [b"a", b"c", b"e"],
    }),
  );
  let is_base = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s: Store| put[d](s, b"a", b"1", Fast));
  let stray = match is_base.value { Ok(s) -> s, Err(_) -> panic("the store rejected") };
  let alone = on_mem[d](is_base.tree, ||
    with_rand(rand(2), || {
      let late: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](stray, b"b", b"2", Fast) };
      error_of(late)
    }).value);
  assert_eq(alone.value, Some(LockLost));
  let beside_another = session(is_base.tree, 3, large_limits(), |s: Store| {
    let late: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](stray, b"b", b"2", Fast) };
    (error_of(late), keys_of(scan[d](snapshot(s), None, None)))
  });
  assert_eq(beside_another.value, Ok((Some(LockLost), [b"a"])))
}

test "a commit the file system failed, or one that finds its next segment started, leaves a store that commits nothing until reopened" {
  let log = "db/00000001.log";
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"1", Fast);
    fs.rename[d](log, "db/aside");
    fs.create_dir[d](log);
    let rejected: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](s1, b"b", b"2", Fast) };
    fs.remove[d](log);
    fs.rename[d]("db/aside", log);
    let retried: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](s1, b"b", b"2", Fast) };
    (error_of(rejected), error_of(retried))
  });
  assert_eq(over.value, Ok((Some(Failed("db/00000001.log", fs::Other(0, "append"))), Some(Stale))));
  let again = session(over.tree, 2, large_limits(), |s: Store|
    shown[d](put[d](s, b"b", b"2", Fast)));
  assert_eq(
    again.value,
    Ok({
      pairs: [{ key: b"a", value: b"1", version: 1 }, { key: b"b", value: b"2", version: 2 }],
      sequence: 2,
    }),
  );
  let planted = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store| {
    let segment_full = put[d](put[d](s0, b"a", b"1".repeat(20), Fast), b"b", b"2".repeat(20), Fast);
    fs.write_file[d]("db/00000002.log", b"another writer's");
    let begun: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
      put[d](segment_full, b"c", b"3", Fast)
    };
    let packed: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { compact[d](segment_full) };
    (error_of(begun), error_of(packed), fs.read_file[d]("db/00000002.log"))
  });
  assert_eq(planted.value, Ok((Some(Stale), Some(Stale), Some(b"another writer's"))))
}

test "a frame written past another writer's fails as stale, and is not a batch to the store opened after" {
  let log = "db/00000001.log";
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"1", Fast);
    let held = fs.read_file[d]("db/lock");
    fs.remove[d]("db/lock");
    close[d](put[d](open[d]("db", large_limits(), Manual, raw(), 1), b"theirs", b"2", Fast));
    fs.write_file[d]("db/lock", match held { Some(bytes) -> bytes, None -> b"" });
    let ours: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { put[d](s1, b"ours", b"3", Fast) };
    (error_of(ours), fs.file_size[d](log))
  });
  assert_eq(over.value, Ok((Some(Stale), Some(213))));
  let again = session(mem_remove(over.tree, "db/lock").fs, 2, large_limits(), |s: Store|
    (shown[d](s), fs.file_size[d](log)));
  assert_eq(
    again.value,
    Ok((
      {
        pairs: [
          { key: b"a", value: b"1", version: 1 },
          { key: b"theirs", value: b"2", version: 2 },
        ],
        sequence: 2,
      },
      Some(163),
    )),
  )
}

/// What happened to a store whose lock a rival requested.
type LockTakeoverOutcome = {
  compaction_error: Option<Error>,
  taken: Bool,
  late_commit_error: Option<Error>,
}

/// The rival `with_rival` runs.
effect contender {
  /// Whether the rival has taken the lock.
  read taken() -> Bool
  /// Lets `ms` pass, after which the rival requests the lock.
  write idle(ms: Int) -> Unit
}

/// Runs `go` on a clock that each ranged read of the root advances by `read_ms`, with a rival that
/// requests the lock after each such read and each `contender.idle`. A whole write of the lock file is
/// stamped with the clock, and every other operation of the root is passed through unchanged.
fn with_rival<a, [l] | e>(
  read_ms: Int,
  go: () -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], contender.taken, contender.idle | e},
) -> a / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l] | e} =
  handle {
    go()
  } with {
    var now = 0,
    var taken = false,
    contender.taken() -> taken,
    contender.idle(ms) -> {
      now = now + ms;
      let had = taken;
      taken = rival_holds[l](now, taken);
      if taken && !had {
        fs.set_modified[l]("db/lock", now);
        ()
      } else { () }
    },
    fs.read_at[l](p, offset, most) -> {
      now = now + read_ms;
      taken = rival_holds[l](now, taken);
      fs.read_at[l](p, offset, most)
    },
    fs.write_file[l](p, body) -> {
      let wrote = fs.write_file[l](p, body);
      if p == "db/lock" {
        fs.set_modified[l](p, now);
        ()
      } else { () };
      wrote
    },
    fs.read_file[l](p) -> fs.read_file[l](p),
    fs.list_dir[l](p) -> fs.list_dir[l](p),
    fs.kind[l](p) -> fs.kind[l](p),
    fs.resolved_kind[l](p) -> fs.resolved_kind[l](p),
    fs.exists[l](p) -> fs.exists[l](p),
    fs.file_size[l](p) -> fs.file_size[l](p),
    fs.modified_ms[l](p) -> fs.modified_ms[l](p),
    fs.append[l](p, body) -> fs.append[l](p, body),
    fs.create_dir[l](p) -> fs.create_dir[l](p),
    fs.remove[l](p) -> fs.remove[l](p),
    fs.rename[l](from, to) -> fs.rename[l](from, to),
    fs.sync[l](p) -> fs.sync[l](p),
    fs.lock[l](p) -> fs.lock[l](p),
    fs.unlock[l](p) -> fs.unlock[l](p),
    fs.copy[l](from, to) -> fs.copy[l](from, to),
    fs.remove_tree[l](p) -> fs.remove_tree[l](p),
    fs.temp_dir[l](dir, prefix) -> fs.temp_dir[l](dir, prefix),
    fs.canonical[l](p) -> fs.canonical[l](p),
    fs.mode[l](p) -> fs.mode[l](p),
    fs.set_mode[l](p, mode) -> fs.set_mode[l](p, mode),
    fs.symlink[l](p, target) -> fs.symlink[l](p, target),
    fs.read_link[l](p) -> fs.read_link[l](p),
    fs.walk[l](p) -> fs.walk[l](p),
    fs.set_modified[l](p, ms) -> fs.set_modified[l](p, ms),
    fs.open[l](p, how) -> fs.open[l](p, how),
    fs.read_chunk[l](file, most) -> fs.read_chunk[l](file, most),
    fs.write_chunk[l](file, body) -> fs.write_chunk[l](file, body),
    fs.close[l](file) -> fs.close[l](file),
    fs.stat[l](p) -> fs.stat[l](p),
    fs.scan[l](p, deep) -> fs.scan[l](p, deep),
    fs.space[l](p) -> fs.space[l](p),
    fs.link[l](p, to) -> fs.link[l](p, to),
  }

/// Whether the rival has the lock after requesting it at `now`, where `taken` reports whether it
/// had it already. A lock file thirty seconds old is the rival's to take.
fn rival_holds<[l]>(now: Int, taken: Bool) -> Bool / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l]} =
  match fs.modified_ms[l]("db/lock") {
    Some(stamp) -> if now - stamp >= 30000 && !taken {
      fs.remove[l]("db/lock");
      fs.write_file[l]("db/lock", b"a rival's token, and its place");
      true
    } else { taken },
    None -> taken,
  }

/// A store of two hundred values that compacts, sits idle for thirty-one seconds and commits once
/// more, on a disk where each ranged read takes `read_ms`. A rival requests the lock after every
/// read, as `fs.lock` would, and a lock file thirty seconds old is the rival's.
fn lock_takeover_outcome(read_ms: Int) -> MemResult<LockTakeoverOutcome> / {abort.raise} =
  on_mem[d](mem_empty(), ||
    with_rand(rand(1), ||
      with_rival[d](read_ms, || {
        let filled: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
          range(0, 200).fold(open[d]("db", large_limits(), Manual, raw(), 1), |s: Store, i: Int|
            put[d](s, Bytes::of_string(i.to_string()), b"value", Fast))
        };
        match filled {
          Err(rejection) ->
            { compaction_error: Some(rejection), taken: false, late_commit_error: None },
          Ok(segment_full) -> {
            let packed: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] { compact[d](segment_full) };
            let taken = contender.taken();
            contender.idle(31000);
            let idle: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
              put[d](match packed { Ok(s) -> s, Err(_) -> segment_full }, b"late", b"l", Fast)
            };
            { compaction_error: error_of(packed), taken: taken, late_commit_error: error_of(idle) }
          },
        }
      })).value)

test "a lock renewed as a compaction copies is never old enough to take, and a store that sat idle past the age has lost it and writes nothing" {
  let is_base = lock_takeover_outcome(400);
  assert_eq(is_base.value, {
    compaction_error: None,
    taken: false,
    late_commit_error: Some(LockLost),
  });
  assert_eq(
    fs::listed_names(mem_list(is_base.tree, "db")),
    Some(["00000001.log", "00000002.hint", "00000002.log", "00000003.log", "lock"]),
  );
  assert_eq(mem_size(is_base.tree, "db/00000003.log"), Some(64));
  let slow = lock_takeover_outcome(600);
  assert_eq(slow.value, {
    compaction_error: Some(LockLost),
    taken: true,
    late_commit_error: Some(LockLost),
  });
  assert_eq(fs::listed_names(mem_list(slow.tree, "db")), Some(["00000001.log", "lock"]));
  assert_eq(mem_read(slow.tree, "db/lock"), Some(b"a rival's token, and its place"))
}

test "values of a type are put and read through their codecs, and a store written under other codecs is rejected entirely" {
  let lists: Codecs<String, List<Int>> = {
    key: bin::string_bin(),
    value: bin::list_bin(bin::int_bin()),
  };
  let counts: Codecs<String, Int> = { key: bin::string_bin(), value: bin::int_bin() };
  let over = opened(mem_empty(), 1, small(), Manual, shape_of(lists), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put_as[d](s0, lists, "primes", [2, 3, 5], Durable);
    let s2 = batch[d](
      s1,
      [put_op(lists, "evens", [0, 2]), Put(key_bytes(lists, "torn"), b"\xff")],
      Fast,
    );
    let torn: Result<Option<List<Int>>, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
      get_as[d](s2, lists, "torn")
    };
    {
      found: [
        get_as[d](s2, lists, "primes"),
        get_as[d](s2, lists, "evens"),
        get_as[d](s2, lists, "odds"),
      ],
      under: listed(prefixed[d](snapshot(s1), key_bytes(lists, "primes"))).map(|p: Pair|
        typed(lists, p)),
      torn: match torn { Err(Undecodable(_)) -> true, _ -> false },
    }
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      found: [Some([2, 3, 5]), Some([0, 2]), None],
      under: [{ key: "primes", value: [2, 3, 5], version: 1 }],
      torn: true,
    }),
  );
  let reopened = |shape: Bytes|
    error_of(opened(over.tree, 2, small(), Manual, shape, |s: Store| ()).value);
  assert_eq(reopened(shape_of(lists)), None);
  assert_eq(reopened(shape_of(counts)), Some(OtherShape));
  assert_eq(reopened(raw()), Some(OtherShape));
  assert(shape_of(lists) != shape_of(counts))
}

test "a compare-and-set commits only against the version it is given, or absence for a key the store does not contain" {
  let over = session(mem_empty(), 1, large_limits(), |s0: Store| {
    let s1 = put[d](s0, b"a", b"1", Fast);
    let won = compare_and_set[d](s1, b"a", Some(1), Some(b"2"), Fast);
    let lost = compare_and_set[d](won.store, b"a", Some(1), Some(b"3"), Fast);
    let state = compare_and_set[d](lost.store, b"b", None, Some(b"new"), Fast);
    let there = compare_and_set[d](state.store, b"b", None, Some(b"again"), Fast);
    let absent = compare_and_set[d](there.store, b"c", Some(1), Some(b"never"), Fast);
    let removed = compare_and_set[d](absent.store, b"a", Some(2), None, Durable);
    (
      [won.done, lost.done, state.done, there.done, absent.done, removed.done],
      shown[d](removed.store),
    )
  });
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok((
      [true, false, true, false, false, true],
      { pairs: [{ key: b"b", value: b"new", version: 3 }], sequence: 4 },
    )),
  )
}

/// One more than the decimal count in `had`, with none counting as zero.
fn one_more(had: Option<Bytes>) -> Bytes / {abort.raise} =
  Bytes::of_string(
    Int::to_string(
      1 + (match had { Some(text) -> text.int_of_ascii().expect("a count"), None -> 0 }),
    ),
  )

/// The store a test's tasks share.
effect newest {
  read store() -> Store
  write replace(s: Store) -> Unit
}

/// Runs `body` with its tasks sharing the store `s` through `newest`, and returns the store once
/// it is done.
fn with_store<a | e>(
  s: Store,
  body: () -> a / {newest.store, newest.replace | e},
) -> { value: a, store: Store } / e =
  handle {
    body()
  } with {
    var held = s,
    newest.store() -> held,
    newest.replace(next) -> {
      held = next;
    },
    return value -> { value: value, store: held },
  }

test "two tasks that add to one count by compare-and-set lose no addition, wherever their reads and commits fall" {
  let over = on_mem[d](mem_empty(), ||
    with_rand(rand(1), || {
      let ran: Result<{ ended: List<Option<Error>>, held: Shown }, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
        let shared = with_store(open[d]("db", large_limits(), Manual, raw(), 1), ||
          simulate {
            let adding = || {
              let tried: Result<Int, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
                [1, 2].fold(0, |_: Int, _: Int|
                  iterate(1, 8, |attempt: Int| {
                    let seen = newest.store();
                    let next = one_more(get[d](seen, b"count"));
                    task.yield();
                    let swapped = compare_and_set[d](
                      newest.store(),
                      b"count",
                      version(seen, b"count"),
                      Some(next),
                      Fast,
                    );
                    newest.replace(swapped.store);
                    if swapped.done { Stop(attempt) } else { Continue(attempt + 1) }
                  }))
              };
              error_of(tried)
            };
            let first = task.spawn(adding);
            let second = task.spawn(adding);
            [task.join(first), task.join(second)]
          });
        close[d](shared.store);
        { ended: shared.value, held: shown[d](shared.store) }
      };
      ran
    }).value);
  assert_eq(
    over.value,
    Ok({
      ended: [None, None],
      held: { pairs: [{ key: b"count", value: b"4", version: 4 }], sequence: 4 },
    }),
  )
}

test "a reader sees all of a batch or none of it, wherever its reads fall among a writer's commits" {
  let over = on_mem[d](mem_empty(), ||
    with_rand(rand(1), || {
      let ran: Result<{ wrote: Option<Error>, read: Result<Bool, Error> }, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
        let shared = with_store(open[d]("db", large_limits(), Manual, raw(), 1), ||
          simulate {
            let writer = task.spawn(|| {
              let wrote: Result<Unit, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
                [b"1", b"2", b"3"].fold((), |_: Unit, value: Bytes| {
                  newest.replace(
                    batch[d](newest.store(), [Put(b"x", value), Put(b"y", value)], Fast),
                  );
                  task.yield()
                })
              };
              error_of(wrote)
            });
            let reader = task.spawn(|| {
              let read: Result<Bool, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
                let snap = snapshot(newest.store());
                task.yield();
                let x = read[d](snap, b"x");
                task.yield();
                x == read[d](snap, b"y")
              };
              read
            });
            { wrote: task.join(writer), read: task.join(reader) }
          });
        close[d](shared.store);
        shared.value
      };
      ran
    }).value);
  assert_eq(over.value, Ok({ wrote: None, read: Ok(true) }))
}

// --- Crashes ----------------------------------------------------------------------

/// A step of a history: one key put, two keys put in one batch, or one key deleted, each with
/// whether it waits for the disk; or a compaction.
type Act = | One(Bool) | Two(Bool) | Drop(Bool) | Compact

/// The batch the step at `i` of a history commits, which is empty for a compaction. Both keys of
/// a batch get the same value, so half a batch would be visible.
fn ops_of(act: Act, i: Int) -> List<Op> = {
  let value = b"value ".concat(byte_of_int(97 + i % 26).repeat(14));
  match act {
    One(_) -> [Put(if i % 2 == 0 { b"a" } else { b"b" }, value)],
    Two(_) -> [Put(b"a", value), Put(b"b", value)],
    Drop(_) -> [Delete(b"a")],
    Compact -> [],
  }
}

/// Whether a power loss keeps a step that returned, as it keeps a compaction.
fn waits(act: Act) -> Bool =
  match act {
    One(durable) -> durable,
    Two(durable) -> durable,
    Drop(durable) -> durable,
    Compact -> true,
  }

fn acted<[l]>(
  s: Store,
  act: Act,
  i: Int,
) -> Store / {fs.read[l], fs.write[l], key_value.fail, entropy.next, abort.raise} =
  match act {
    Compact -> compact[l](s),
    _ -> batch[l](s, ops_of(act, i), if waits(act) { Durable } else { Fast }),
  }

/// What a map contains after each number of a history's steps, starting with none.
fn contents_after_each(acts: List<Act>) -> List<Shown> / {abort.raise} =
  acts.fold([{ pairs: [], sequence: 0 }], |all: List<Shown>, act: Act|
    all.push(stepped(all.at(all.len() - 1).expect("the last"), ops_of(act, all.len() - 1))))

/// How many steps of a history returned on a disk that accepts `writes` more writes and then
/// fails, along with the tree the run left.
fn completed_actions(
  acts: List<Act>,
  limits: Limits,
  writes: Int,
) -> MemResult<Int> / {abort.raise} =
  on_mem_failing[d](mem_empty(), writes, ||
    with_rand(rand(1), || {
      let ran: Result<Int, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
        with_scope[store](open[d]("db", limits, Manual, raw(), 1), |s: Store|
          close[d](s)) { held ->
          iterate(
            { store: held.get(), done: 0 },
            acts.len() + 1,
            |at: { store: Store, done: Int }|
              match acts.at(at.done) {
                None -> Stop(at.done),
                Some(act) -> {
                  let next: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
                    acted[d](at.store, act, at.done)
                  };
                  match next {
                    Ok(store) -> Continue({ store: store, done: at.done + 1 }),
                    Err(_) -> Stop(at.done),
                  }
                },
              },
          )
        }
      };
      match ran { Ok(done) -> done, Err(_) -> 0 }
    }).value)

/// Whether the tree a dead store left opens, once its lock is gone, to what a map contains after
/// some number of steps from `least` to `most`, accepts a commit, and opens again with both.
fn revives(
  tree: MemFs,
  limits: Limits,
  all: List<Shown>,
  least: Int,
  most: Int,
) -> Bool / {abort.raise} = {
  let first = session(mem_remove(tree, "db/lock").fs, 2, limits, |s: Store| {
    let found = shown[d](s);
    put[d](s, b"z", b"after", Durable);
    found
  });
  match first.value {
    Err(_) -> false,
    Ok(found) ->
      range(least, most + 1).any(|count: Int| all.at(count) == Some(found))
        && session(first.tree, 3, limits, |s: Store| shown[d](s)).value
        == Ok(stepped(found, [Put(b"z", b"after")])),
  }
}

/// Kills a run at each number of writes in turn, up to the number that lets it finish and close.
/// Returns how many numbers that is, and the first, if any, whose tree `holds` returns false for.
/// `holds` is called on each tree as the run left it, and as a power loss then leaves it, which
/// its last argument indicates, along with what the run returned.
fn crashes(
  whole: Int,
  run: (Int) -> MemResult<Int> / {abort.raise},
  holds: (MemFs, Int, Bool) -> Bool / {abort.raise},
) -> { points: Int, broken: Option<Int> } / {abort.raise} =
  iterate(0, 100000, |writes: Int| {
    let ran = run(writes);
    if !holds(ran.tree, ran.value, false) || !holds(mem_crashed(ran.tree), ran.value, true) {
      Stop({ points: writes + 1, broken: Some(writes) })
    } else if ran.value == whole && !mem_exists(ran.tree, "db/lock") {
      Stop({ points: writes + 1, broken: None })
    } else { Continue(writes + 1) }
  })

/// How many of the first `done` steps a power loss must keep: those up to the last one that
/// waited for the disk.
fn durable_prefix_length(acts: List<Act>, done: Int) -> Int =
  range(0, done).fold(0, |is_base: Int, i: Int|
    match acts.at(i) { Some(act) -> if waits(act) { i + 1 } else { is_base }, None -> is_base })

/// Kills a history at each of its writes. Opening what is left gives a number of its steps from
/// those that had returned, or after a power loss those that had to be kept, up to one more.
fn check_crash_recovery(
  acts: List<Act>,
  limits: Limits,
) -> { points: Int, broken: Option<Int> } / {abort.raise} = {
  let all = contents_after_each(acts);
  crashes(
    acts.len(),
    |writes: Int| completed_actions(acts, limits, writes),
    |tree: MemFs, done: Int, lost_power: Bool|
      revives(
        tree,
        limits,
        all,
        if lost_power { durable_prefix_length(acts, done) } else { done },
        min(done + 1, acts.len()),
      ),
  )
}

law "a store that dies at any write of three steps opens to a prefix of them, with every step that returned and, after a loss of power, every durable one"
  forall (first: Act, second: Act, third: Act) {
    check_crash_recovery([first, second, third], small()).broken == None
  }

/// A generator of `Act` for tests.
fn acts() -> gen::Gen<Act> =
  gen::one_of([
    gen::map(gen::bool(), |f0: Bool| One(f0)),
    gen::map(gen::bool(), |f0: Bool| Two(f0)),
    gen::map(gen::bool(), |f0: Bool| Drop(f0)),
    gen::constant(Compact),
  ])

/// A generator of `Step` for tests.
fn steps() -> gen::Gen<Step> =
  gen::one_of([
    gen::map2(names(), gen::any_int(), |f0: Name, f1: Int| Set(f0, f1)),
    gen::map(names(), |f0: Name| Unset(f0)),
    gen::map3(names(), names(), gen::any_int(), |f0: Name, f1: Name, f2: Int| Both(f0, f1, f2)),
    gen::constant(Tidy),
  ])

/// A generator of `Name` for tests.
fn names() -> gen::Gen<Name> = gen::elements([Ka, Kb, Kc])

test "a longer history that dies at a random write opens to a prefix with everything it must keep" {
  gen::check2(gen::list(acts()), gen::any_int(), |drawn: List<Act>, at: Int| {
    let acts = drawn.take(16);
    let all = contents_after_each(acts);
    let span = 24 + 14 * acts.len();
    let ran = completed_actions(acts, small(), (at % span + span) % span);
    let most = min(ran.value + 1, acts.len());
    revives(ran.tree, small(), all, ran.value, most)
      && revives(mem_crashed(ran.tree), small(), all, durable_prefix_length(acts, ran.value), most)
  })
}

test "a compaction that dies at any of its writes, a renewal of its lock among them, leaves every value to the store opened after it" {
  let limits: Limits = { key: 8, value: 64, batch: 96, file: 1024 };
  let state = session(mem_empty(), 1, limits, |s0: Store|
    shown[d](
      range(0, 70).fold(s0, |s: Store, i: Int|
        put[d](s, Bytes::of_string(Int::to_string(100 + i)), b"v", if i == 69 { Durable }
        else { Fast })),
    ));
  let held = match state.value { Ok(found) -> found, Err(_) -> panic("the store rejected") };
  assert_eq(held.pairs.len(), 70);
  let died = crashes(
    1,
    |writes: Int|
      on_mem_failing[d](state.tree, writes, ||
        with_rand(rand(4), || {
          let ran: Result<Store, Error> = try[key_value.fail] {
            with_scope[store](open[d]("db", limits, Manual, raw(), 1), |s: Store|
              close[d](s)) { held -> compact[d](held.get()) }
          };
          match ran { Ok(_) -> 1, Err(_) -> 0 }
        }).value),
    |tree: MemFs, _: Int, _: Bool| revives(tree, limits, [held], 0, 0),
  );
  assert_eq(died.broken, None);
  assert(died.points > 20)
}

// --- A store against a map ----------------------------------------------------------

type Name = | Ka | Kb | Kc

/// A step of a random history: a put, a delete, two puts in one batch, or a compaction.
type Step = | Set(Name, Int) | Unset(Name) | Both(Name, Name, Int) | Tidy

fn name_of(name: Name) -> Bytes = match name { Ka -> b"a", Kb -> b"b", Kc -> b"c" }

fn step_ops(step: Step) -> List<Op> =
  match step {
    Set(name, n) -> [Put(name_of(name), Bytes::of_string(n.to_string()))],
    Unset(name) -> [Delete(name_of(name))],
    Both(one, other, n) -> {
      let text = Bytes::of_string(n.to_string());
      [Put(name_of(one), text), Put(name_of(other), text.concat(b"'"))]
    },
    Tidy -> [],
  }

/// What a store shows after a history, each step one batch or one compaction.
fn after(steps: List<Step>) -> Shown / {abort.raise} = {
  let ran = session(mem_empty(), 1, small(), |s0: Store|
    shown[d](
      steps.fold(s0, |s: Store, step: Step|
        match step { Tidy -> compact[d](s), _ -> batch[d](s, step_ops(step), Fast) }),
    ));
  match ran.value { Ok(found) -> found, Err(_) -> panic("the store rejected") }
}

fn append_step(steps: List<Step>, step: Step) -> List<Step> = steps.push(step)

fn as_a_map(held: Shown, step: Step) -> Shown = stepped(held, step_ops(step))

test "a store changes its contents as a map does, with a version being its batch's number and a compaction changing nothing" {
  gen::check2(gen::list(steps()), steps(), |x: List<Step>, y: Step|
    refines(after, append_step, as_a_map, x, y))
}
